window.__hvBdRegister("proclus-theology", {"key":"proclus-theology","sections":[{"i":0,"j":14,"loc":"Introduction (1 of 8)","title":"The translator's case for Platonic theology","summary":"The translator presents Plato's theology as handed down from Orpheus through Pythagoras to Plato, and praises it for honouring the first cause as beyond being, called the one and the good only through human weakness. He sets out its reasoning: since the principle of all is one, and producers first generate what is like themselves, the one must first produce a multitude of unities, the Gods, from which being, life, intellect, soul, nature and body depend. He then argues that a divine multitude under one supreme God is admitted by all nations, including the Old Testament, citing the Septuagint of Deuteronomy, Job, the Psalms and a Jewish rabbi to show that the stars were treated as animated Gods allotted to the nations. He closes with Julian's argument that national differences in law and custom require ethnarchic Gods.","ideas":["The first principle is the principle of principles, beyond being and all names","Like produces like first, so the one generates the Gods before anything else","Scripture is read as granting animated star-Gods to every nation except Israel","Julian: differing nations need different presiding powers"],"terms":[{"t":"the one / the good","d":"Names for the ineffable first principle, marking its simplicity and its being desired by all things."},{"t":"monad","d":"A leading unity that heads a series extending from itself down to the last of things."}]},{"i":15,"j":32,"loc":"Introduction (2 of 8)","title":"Witnesses that the heavens are animated","summary":"The translator lists Church Fathers and schoolmen, among them Jerome, Origen, Augustine, Aquinas and Cusanus, who held that the heavenly bodies have souls. He gives Synesius' hymns in Greek and English, which praise the intellectual Gods, starry intellects and the powers ruling Thrace and Chalcedon. He then quotes Kepler on a world soul seated in the sun and on the earth as an animal, and Berkeley's Siris on ancient belief in an animated, mind-governed world. He ends by preferring this view to Newton's, claiming that planets move by a living tendency toward the sun rather than by external force.","ideas":["Christian authorities are cited as holding that the stars have souls","Synesius is called the wisest of the ancient Christians","Kepler and Berkeley are enlisted for a world soul","Planetary motion is presented as natural desire rather than violence"],"terms":[{"t":"zonic and azonic Gods","d":"Chaldean orders of Gods, the first of the stars, the second the liberated order above them."}]},{"i":33,"j":45,"loc":"Introduction (3 of 8)","title":"Paul, the aeones, and Dionysius' hierarchy","summary":"The translator argues that Paul distinguished Gods from angels. He renders Hebrews 11:3 so that the aeones, the invisible exemplars of visible things known to the Valentinians and Chaldeans, are framed by God's word, and he takes Paul's principalities, powers and dominions, ranked above angels and Christ, as Gods. He cites 1 Corinthians 8:5, Gods many and Lords many, with Pseudo-Dionysius' gloss that the many Gods remain united in the one. He then argues that Dionysius, drawing on Proclus, refashioned the Platonic orders of Gods into the nine angelic choirs, Seraphim to Angels.","ideas":["The aeones are read as divine exemplars, akin to Plato's ideas","Principalities and powers outrank angels and so must be Gods","Divine unities are rooted in the one as trees by their roots","Dionysius' celestial hierarchy is said to copy Proclus' divine orders"],"terms":[{"t":"aiōn (aeon)","d":"Taken here as an invisible divine exemplar of the visible world, not a world or an age."},{"t":"archai (principalities)","d":"Proclus' name for the supermundane Gods, which the translator finds in Paul and Dionysius."}]},{"i":46,"j":59,"loc":"Introduction (4 of 8)","title":"Against worshipping men as Gods","summary":"The translator holds that Platonic theology in its purity never deified dead men, citing Plato's Laws, the Golden Verses with Hierocles, and Plutarch's attack on Euemerus, who turned all Gods into ancient kings, and on those who reduce Gods to wine, flame or crops. Julian is quoted on Christians taking blasphemy from the Hebrews and irreverence from the heathens, and Stillingfleet on Callisthenes and the Greeks who refused to prostrate themselves before kings. Sallust says those who honour kings as Gods are punished by losing the Gods. The translator blames such deification on forgetting the first God's transcendence, which Simplicius says no conception can reach.","ideas":["Gods, heroes and good men each receive their own rank of honour","Euemerism and turning Gods into natural things both lead to atheism","Callisthenes refused divine honours to Alexander","Forgetting the first God's transcendence led to deifying men"],"terms":[{"t":"Euemerus","d":"Author of a fictitious history making the Gods into ancient generals and kings, blamed by Plutarch for spreading atheism."}]},{"i":60,"j":65,"loc":"Introduction (5 of 8)","title":"Statues as aids to remembering the Gods","summary":"The translator argues that intelligent heathens treated statues as likenesses and reminders of the Gods, not as Gods. Sallust explains that temples, altars, statues, prayers and sacrifices produce a fitness to receive divine gifts and a conjunction with the Gods, who gain nothing from them; life, offered in sacrifice, is the medium joining human life to divine life. Maximus Tyrius compares statues to letters, or to marks a writing master gives children to trace, and defends the Greek use of the human form. He ends by tolerating all nations' different symbols, so long as people know, love and remember the deity they honour.","ideas":["Worship works by likeness, producing fitness to receive the Gods","Sacrifice supplies life as the medium between human and divine life","Statues are aids to memory for the many, unneeded by the rare few","The human form is the fittest image of the divine"],"terms":[{"t":"habitude (fitness)","d":"The aptitude produced by likeness through which souls receive the Gods' gifts."}]},{"i":66,"j":74,"loc":"Introduction (6 of 8)","title":"Sacred animals, vestments, and Julian on images","summary":"Plutarch, from On Isis and Osiris, defends Egyptian animal worship: some animals were honoured for their use, others as faint images of divine power, such as the beetle mirroring the sun's course and the tongueless crocodile figuring the silent divine reason. He compares Greek symbols and Pythagorean names for numbers, and argues that living beings image the divine better than lifeless gold or stone. Isis' many-coloured robes signify matter, Osiris' single bright one the intelligible, and Osiris is said to lead released souls. Julian then argues that statues are symbols through which the Gods are worshipped, as subjects honour a king's image, and that Christians wrongly think pagans take statues for Gods.","ideas":["Animals are honoured for usefulness or as images of divine powers","Living things image the divine better than any lifeless material","Isis' varied robes signify matter, Osiris' single robe the intelligible","Images of Gods are honoured as subjects honour the king's image"],"terms":[{"t":"epoptic","d":"The intuitive, culminating part of philosophy in which the soul touches the simple principle directly."}]},{"i":75,"j":82,"loc":"Introduction (7 of 8)","title":"Catholic saints, and the work itself","summary":"The translator draws on the Church of England's Homilies, which liken the cult of Catholic saints, with patrons for cities, trades, diseases and livestock and votive offerings at images, to pagan worship, and which judge it worse. He concludes that pagan religion in its pure form, honouring the immediate offspring of the ineffable, is preferable. Turning to the treatise, he calls it a scientific unfolding of the divine processions, quotes Plato's Second and Seventh Letters that such things seem ridiculous to the many, and says Proclus disclosed them as the schools were about to be destroyed. He praises Proclus' style, quoting Kepler.","ideas":["Saints are presented as substitutes for the Gods presiding over places and crafts","The Homilies are cited as ranking pagans above Catholics","Plato says these doctrines can only be kindled by long familiarity","Proclus' diction is called pure, copious and magnificent"],"terms":[]},{"i":83,"j":97,"loc":"Introduction (8 of 8)","title":"Life of Proclus; the translation","summary":"Following Marinus, the translator summarises the life of Proclus: born at Byzantium in 412 to Lycian parents, raised at Xanthus, gifted in body and mind, trained at Alexandria under Olympiodorus and Hero, then at Athens under Syrianus. There he read all of Aristotle in under two years before being led into Plato, and wrote his Timaeus commentary by twenty-eight. Marinus describes his virtues rising to theurgic ones, his tireless lecturing and prayers to the sun, a light seen round his head, rain and earthquake charms, verses from dreams and his belief that he had Nicomachus' soul. The translator then explains that he added a seventh book and the Elements of Theology, and corrected over four hundred faults in the Greek.","ideas":["Syrianus led Proclus from Aristotle to Plato in order","Proclus rose through political and cathartic to higher virtues","Marinus reports wonders and prophetic verses","A seventh book is supplied by the translator from other works of Proclus"],"terms":[{"t":"Platonic Successor","d":"The title given to Proclus as head of the Athenian school after Syrianus."}]},{"i":98,"j":144,"loc":"Endnotes (1 of 2)","title":"Endnotes: sources and textual notes","summary":"The endnotes give Greek originals and references for passages quoted in the introduction: Maximus Tyrius, Simplicius, Pseudo-Dionysius, Sallust and Damascius. The translator adds that Synesius wrongly calls zonic and azonic Gods angels, quotes Gregory and Horrox's high praise of Kepler while calling it excessive, and gives Kepler's Latin. One note relates the aeones to the intellectual order and the Demiurgus who absorbs Phanes. Others cite Stillingfleet on the distinction between heroic and divine honours, the Egyptian thrice unknown darkness, Isidorus approaching the Gods by unknown love, and several emendations of Plutarch's text.","ideas":["The heathens distinguished second honours to heroes from those due to Gods","The Egyptians called the first principle a thrice unknown darkness","Isidorus sought the Gods themselves through an ineffable love","Several textual corrections to Plutarch are proposed"],"terms":[{"t":"Synoches","d":"The second triad of the intelligible and at the same time intellectual order of Gods."}]},{"i":145,"j":167,"loc":"Endnotes (2 of 2)","title":"Endnotes: statues, Brahmins, and metempsychosis","summary":"Further notes give Stillingfleet's summary of Dio Chrysostom, who has Phidias argue that men represent the invisible God through the human form, and Bernier's account of Benares Brahmins who say they pray to the being a statue represents, not the statue. The translator notes that the ineffable principle is beyond self-subsistence, so many media must join us to it. Notes to the Life explain the thyrsus-bearers, the Hecatic sphere, and the Pythagorean transmigration, which Proclus explains as a human soul bound to a brutal life by affinity rather than entering a brute body. A last note argues from Proclus' promise about divine names that a seventh book once existed.","ideas":["Images exist because people want the divine near and present","The supreme principle is all things prior to all","The soul joins a brutal life, not a brutal body","Book I's promise about divine names shows a lost book"],"terms":[{"t":"metempsychosis","d":"Transmigration of souls, here explained as a soul's attachment to a lower life by likeness of affection."}]},{"i":168,"j":178,"loc":"Book I, Chapters I–II","title":"Proclus' prologue and the qualified reader","summary":"Addressing Pericles, Proclus says Plato's philosophy came to light by the will of higher powers, later withdrew, and shone forth again through Plotinus, Amelius, Porphyry, Iamblichus, Theodorus, and finally his own teacher, whom he thanks. He invokes the Gods as leaders of the inquiry. He divides the work into three parts: common conceptions of the Gods, the total orders of Gods, and the particular Gods named in Plato. The reader must have the moral virtues, training in logic, analysis and division, knowledge of physics, and a love for the subject, since Plato says no better helper than love can be found.","ideas":["Plato's theology passed down through a succession of interpreters","Any account of the Gods must begin by invoking the Gods","Symbols are rendered clear and images referred to their exemplars","The hearer needs virtue, dialectic, physics and love"],"terms":[{"t":"epopteia","d":"The mystic vision of the highest rites, used for Plato's highest doctrines about the Gods."}]},{"i":179,"j":194,"loc":"Book I, Chapters III–IV","title":"What theology is and its modes","summary":"Proclus says all theologians call the first principles Gods, but some take them as bodies, others as souls, others as intellect; Plato alone rises above body, soul and intellect to an ineffable unity and its multitude of unities. Theology concerns these unities, which are known not by sense, opinion or intellect but by the soul's own one, as the Alcibiades suggests when the soul turns inward to see the Gods. He then distinguishes Plato's modes: inspired (Phaedrus), dialectical (Sophist, Parmenides), symbolic (myths of the Gorgias, Symposium, Protagoras) and through images (Timaeus, Statesman). These match Orphic symbols, Pythagorean images, inspired initiators and Plato's own science. Plato accepts myths that make the divine good and unchanging and rejects tragic myths and physical allegory.","ideas":["Plato's three monads above bodies: soul, intellect, and the unity above intellect","Like is known by like, so the Gods are known by the one in the soul","Four modes of theology: symbolic, through images, inspired and scientific","Myths must keep a hidden meaning more venerable than the apparent one"],"terms":[{"t":"hyparxis","d":"The highest summit of a thing's existence, its unity, by which the soul can touch the Gods."},{"t":"entheastic","d":"Speaking under divine inspiration, as Socrates does in the Phaedrus."}]},{"i":195,"j":208,"loc":"Book I, Chapters V–VII","title":"Which dialogues teach theology; the Parmenides","summary":"Proclus says the truth about the Gods runs through every dialogue, but ranks first the Phaedo, Phaedrus, Symposium, Philebus, Sophist, Statesman, Cratylus and Timaeus, then the myths of the Gorgias and Protagoras, the Laws on providence and Republic X, and third the Letters, assigning specific divine orders to each. He traces Greek theology to Orpheus through Pythagoras and Plato. He then states an objection: gathering scattered passages yields no single whole account, and Plato's myths and theological passages serve ethical or physical ends. His reply is that the Parmenides gives the whole procession of the divine genera from the first cause, and that the other dialogues' parts should be referred to it, as the Timaeus contains the whole science of nature.","ideas":["Each dialogue is assigned particular orders of Gods","All Greek theology descends from Orphic mystic tradition","An objection: scattered dogmas make no complete theology","The Parmenides contains the complete generation of the Gods"],"terms":[{"t":"bound and infinity","d":"The two principles after the one, which the Philebus refers to the Pythagoreans."}]},{"i":209,"j":220,"loc":"Book I, Chapters VIII–IX","title":"The Parmenides is not a logical exercise","summary":"Proclus names two groups of opponents: those who see the Parmenides as mere exercise in opposed arguments, and others who refer its hypotheses to the first God, intellect and lower beings but will not divide the many orders of Gods. Against the first, he argues that Parmenides treats the method as a serious contest, that an aged lover of truth would not trifle with words, and that doxastic argument does not suit true being. Plato elsewhere fits each speaker's discourse to his teaching, and Socrates in the Theaetetus and Sophist praises Parmenides' noble depth. Plato's dialectic, as described in the Republic, rises through divisions and analyses to the good, unlike merely opinion-based reasoning.","ideas":["Two rival readings of the Parmenides are set out","Opinion-based argument cannot treat intelligible being","Socrates testifies to Parmenides' depth","True dialectic ascends to the unhypothetical good"],"terms":[{"t":"doxastic","d":"Belonging to opinion, unstable and various, as opposed to intellectual knowledge."},{"t":"dialectic","d":"Plato's method of division, analysis, definition and demonstration that leads to the first principles."}]},{"i":221,"j":228,"loc":"Book I, Chapter X","title":"The second hypothesis unfolds many divine orders","summary":"Proclus turns to the harder opponents, who agree the Parmenides is about first causes. Of its nine hypotheses, five assume that the one exists and four that it does not; all agree the first concerns the ineffable principle above being. Plotinus' followers read the second as a single intellectual nature, but Proclus' teacher distributed its conclusions across the divine orders, first to first, middle to middle, last to last. Proclus argues that being is many, as heaven and soul contain ordered multitudes, that the Philebus requires division into monads and numbers, and that the order of demonstrations, simple ones first and composite later, mirrors an order of causes.","ideas":["Nine hypotheses, five positing the one and four denying it","Each conclusion of the second hypothesis signals a divine order","The dividing method forbids bringing all multitude at once to the one","The order of demonstrations images an order of causes"],"terms":[{"t":"hypothesis","d":"One of the nine arguments of the Parmenides, each read as treating a different level of reality."}]},{"i":229,"j":241,"loc":"Book I, Chapter XI","title":"Further proofs from the conclusions' order","summary":"Proclus argues that the conclusions about true being cannot all fit the whole alone, nor each part alike, so different conclusions belong to different orders. Since the second hypothesis begins from being characterized by the one and ends with what participates of time, it runs from first to last; and the hypotheses run from the one to true beings to soul. He cites Plotinus on being prior to number, Porphyry on something in intellect prior to the eternal, and Iamblichus on the genera of being placed at the end of the intellectual order. Only this reading explains the fourteen conclusions' number, order and dependence, and why the one is unseparated from being until the fourth. The second hypothesis therefore unfolds all the divine orders, from the most unified to the last.","ideas":["Different conclusions must belong to different levels of being","Plotinus, Porphyry and Iamblichus support a graded order","The fourteen conclusions' number and order need explaining","Higher orders get fewer arguments, being nearer the ineffable"],"terms":[{"t":"one being","d":"Being characterized by the one, the first conclusion of the second hypothesis and the summit of intelligibles."}]},{"i":242,"j":253,"loc":"Book I, Chapters XII–XIII","title":"Parmenides' hypotheses; proof that Gods exist begins","summary":"Proclus finishes his account of the Parmenides: the first hypothesis denies everything of the first principle, the second unfolds the whole order of the Gods, the third partial souls, the fourth what is inseparable from bodies, the fifth matter, while the four remaining hypotheses show that without the one nothing at all exists. He then turns to Plato's common dogmas about divine things, beginning with three from the Laws: that Gods exist, that their providence reaches all things, and that they govern justly and cannot be perverted. The proof that Gods exist starts from a ranking of motion: things moved only, things moved by another that move others, self-moving things, and the immoveable. Bodies are only moved, qualities and forms in bodies move only through another, and so self-motion must belong to soul, the source of motion for animated bodies.","ideas":["The hypotheses of the Parmenides map the whole range of beings from the Gods down to matter","The Laws give three first theological dogmas: existence, providence, incorruptible justice of the Gods","Motion is ranked: moved only, alter-motive, self-motive, immoveable","Soul is the primary self-moving nature that gives bodies the image of self-motion"],"terms":[{"t":"hyparxis","d":"Proclus' word for the highest mode of a thing's existence, its very subsistence or summit as one."},{"t":"alter-motive","d":"Moved by something else, as opposed to self-motive."}]},{"i":254,"j":262,"loc":"Book I, Chapter XIII","title":"From soul to intellect to divinity","summary":"Since soul is older than body and moves it, the heaven and its bodies must have ruling souls, and since the heaven moves in ordered, uniform revolution, these souls must be rational, not irrational. A soul that is rational by participation points to an intellect prior to soul that is intellect by essence, and the heaven's sameness of motion itself shows intellect at work above soul. Intellect in turn is deified by the one prior to it, so body, soul, intellect and divinity form a chain in which each depends on what is above it. Proclus extends the same reasoning to each heavenly sphere and the elements, concluding that there is one soul, intellect and god of the universe with many under each, and then announces the next dogma of the Laws, providence.","ideas":["Ordered celestial motion requires an intellectual, not an irrational, soul","Soul has intellect by participation, so an essential intellect must precede it","Intellect is divine through the one, as soul is intellectual through its summit (citing Plotinus)","Each perpetual part of the world is ensouled and intellectual like the whole"],"terms":[{"t":"immoveable intellect","d":"Intellect's stillness is not lifeless inertia but the source and fountain of all motion and life."}]},{"i":263,"j":276,"loc":"Book I, Chapter XIV","title":"The providence of the Gods over all","summary":"Proclus argues that because the Gods are causes of all motion, being and life, and because everything down to matter participates of the one, all things must fall under their providence. He runs through the alternatives: if the Gods neglected their offspring it would be through ignorance, lack of power, or lack of will, and each is impossible for beings who are causes of beauty and good, who produce even the smallest things, and whose being is defined by goodness. Their providence is not laborious or busy like human administration, but spontaneous, immaterial and ineffable, joining production by their very being with will. The distinctive Platonic teaching is that the Gods remain separate and unmixed while still extending care to all, each order of Gods providing in its own way.","ideas":["Offspring must enjoy the providential care of their causes","Neglect would imply ignorance, impotence or ill will, all incompatible with Gods","Divine providence is effortless, as heating is natural to fire","The Gods are both wholly transcendent and present to all things"],"terms":[{"t":"pronoia (providence)","d":"The Gods' care for what they produce, exercised by their very being and goodness."}]},{"i":277,"j":282,"loc":"Book I, Chapters XV–XVI","title":"Divine justice cannot be bribed","summary":"The third dogma of the Laws is that the Gods govern by justice and cannot be turned aside by gifts. Using the pilot, physician, general and guardian as examples, Proclus argues that every true ruler aims at the good of the ruled, so the Gods, being wholly good, cannot be inferior to human leaders or favor the vicious who worship them. Otherwise the universe would be full of disorder, and earthly gifts would outrank Olympian goods. He concludes that existence, universal providence and unchanging justice form a triad found in all the Gods, and then turns to the Republic, where Socrates lays down laws for poets: the Gods cause good and no evil, and they are unchanging and free of deception, which rests on their goodness, immutability and truth.","ideas":["Every ruler by nature seeks the good of those ruled; the Gods most of all","Gods swayed by offerings would make vice more honourable than virtue","Hyparxis, providence and undeviating intellect are common to all Gods","The Republic's theological types: Gods cause no evil and are unchanging and truthful"],"terms":[{"t":"theological types","d":"The rules Socrates in Republic II sets for poets' tales about the Gods."}]},{"i":283,"j":290,"loc":"Book I, Chapter XVII","title":"The Gods cause all good, no evil","summary":"Because the being of the Gods is defined by the good, Proclus argues, they produce every good and nothing contrary to it, just as a cause of life does not cause death. Evil arises not from divine power but from the weakness of recipients at the lowest levels, which cannot keep the gift pure. Wholes are always free of evil, and what is evil to a part is good to the universe; evil never exists wholly unmixed with good and is put to good use, as justice purifies souls. Things receive good according to their capacity, as things receive sunlight differently, and Proclus rejects any evil principle, malefic soul or paradigm of evil as foreign to Plato.","ideas":["The primary good gives rise only to goods","Evil comes from the weakness of partial, material recipients","No evil exists in wholes; corruption of a part serves the whole","Plato admits no evil cause or principle at war with the good"],"terms":[{"t":"parhypostasis (shadowy subsistence)","d":"Evil's derivative, parasitic existence, lacking a cause of its own."}]},{"i":291,"j":298,"loc":"Book I, Chapter XVIII","title":"The immutability and self-sufficiency of the Gods","summary":"Following the Republic, Proclus holds that each God is most excellent in his own order and so neither changes for the worse nor for the better. Against those who say only the first cause is best, he argues that each God has a primary excellence in his own peculiarity, as Timæus calls the demiurgus the best of causes. He then distinguishes the self-sufficiency, impassivity and sameness of the world, of divine souls, and of intellect, showing each falls short of the Gods' own: the world's is composite, souls' unfolds in time, and intellect's contains difference. Only the Gods are self-sufficient through themselves and immutable as pure unities.","ideas":["Each God is supreme within his own series","Immutability entails self-sufficiency, purity and sameness","World, soul and intellect possess these qualities only derivatively","Divine unities deify their participants while receiving nothing from them"],"terms":[{"t":"autarkeia (self-sufficiency)","d":"Lacking nothing; in the Gods it belongs by their very being, not by participation."}]},{"i":299,"j":308,"loc":"Book I, Chapters XIX–XX","title":"The simplicity and truth of the Gods","summary":"Proclus explains divine simplicity by contrasting it with lesser kinds: numerical unity, species and genera, nature, soul and even intellect, which is a one-many. The Gods are simple beyond all multitude, though the images they send forth appear multiform, as in the mysteries and the Phædrus' vision of simple, motionless spectacles. Divine truth likewise surpasses the truth of words, of opinion and science, and of intellect: it is the Gods' undivided union, by which they know all things at once, even particulars, contingents and matter. False oracles are due to recipients, instruments, places or times, not to the Gods, and truth is the power that unites what fills with what is filled.","ideas":["Intellect is one-many; only the Gods are simply one","Multiform appearances come from uniform Gods","Divine knowledge grasps wholes and parts, eternal and temporal, immediately","Errors in oracles arise from the recipients, not the Gods"],"terms":[{"t":"alētheia (truth)","d":"In the Gods, the union that binds knower and known, more than truth in words or minds."}]},{"i":309,"j":316,"loc":"Book I, Chapters XXI–XXIII","title":"Divine goodness and wisdom","summary":"From the Phædrus Proclus takes the triad that everything divine is beautiful, wise and good, and treats it in order. Goodness, drawing on the Philebus, has three elements: the desirable, which draws all things toward it like the sun turning the eye; the sufficient, which carries the Gods' gifts outward and moves all things to generate; and the perfect, which turns progressions back to their causes. Wisdom comes second: from the Banquet, Republic and Theætetus Proclus gathers that divine wisdom is full of what it knows, generates truth and intellect, and perfects imperfect natures.","ideas":["Everything divine is good, wise and beautiful","Goodness as desirable, sufficient and perfect: abiding, procession, return","All beings dance around the unknowable desirable","Divine wisdom is full, generative and perfective; no God philosophizes"],"terms":[{"t":"to epheton (the desirable)","d":"The good as the object of all desire, around which all things are established."},{"t":"hikanon (the sufficient)","d":"The good's overflowing power that begins procession and generation."}]},{"i":317,"j":324,"loc":"Book I, Chapters XXIV–XXV","title":"Divine beauty; faith, truth and love","summary":"Divine beauty, Proclus says, subsists in the intelligible and gives the Gods their loveliness and their mutual joy and friendship. He gathers three marks of it from Plato: delicate (Banquet), splendid (Phædrus), and the object of love. Three uniting powers correspond to the triad: love joins things to beauty, truth to wisdom, and faith to the good. Faith here is not belief about sensibles or assent to common notions, but a quiet, uniform resting in the good beyond all knowledge, which unites Gods, dæmons and happy souls to it. He finds support in the Laws' links between truth, trustworthiness and friendship, and names three ways of salvation: love, philosophy and theurgy.","ideas":["Beauty is delicate, splendid and lovable","Love, truth and faith join the filled to beauty, wisdom and goodness","Faith surpasses knowledge and rests in the good","Salvation through erotic madness, philosophy and theurgic power"],"terms":[{"t":"pistis (faith)","d":"A union with the good above cognition, not opinion or belief."},{"t":"theurgy","d":"Divinely empowered ritual action, which Proclus ranks above human wisdom."}]},{"i":325,"j":330,"loc":"Book I, Chapter XXVI","title":"The divine, the immortal, the intelligible","summary":"Turning to the Phædo, where the essence the soul resembles is called divine, immortal, intelligible, uniform, indissoluble and unchanging, Proclus examines each term. A God proper is a superessential unity; the divine is being that participates the one. Plato also calls divine souls, dæmons and even men Gods, so Proclus distinguishes Gods simply, by union, by participation, by contact and by similitude. He ranks kinds of immortality from the renewed immortality of heavenly bodies up to that of the Gods, the source of all life, and orders kinds of intelligible from soul to divine hyparxis, so that the divine, immortal and intelligible form a descending triad.","ideas":["The divine is being that participates the one; a God is the one itself in each order","Five grades of being a God: simply, by union, participation, contact, similitude","Immortality ranges from celestial bodies to the Gods","Divine, immortal and intelligible correspond to deified being, life and intellect"],"terms":[{"t":"superessential unities","d":"The Gods as henads, above being, measuring and generating essences."}]},{"i":331,"j":341,"loc":"Book I, Chapters XXVII–XXIX","title":"Unbegotten Gods, fathers, mothers, divine names","summary":"Proclus pairs uniform, indissoluble and unchanging with divine, immortal and intelligible, citing Timæus' contrast of dissoluble and immortal. He holds that the Gods are unbegotten, and that the births of Gods in myths, such as Venus and Love in Diotima's tale or Time in the Orphic writings, symbolize procession from causes; Plato mythologizes with the theologians but speaks of unbegotten Gods when reasoning dialectically. Fathers in myth stand for the monad and bound, mothers for the duad and infinite power, and their offspring take after one or the other. Finally, the first names belong to the Gods, second ones to dæmons, and human names are images, made like statues of the Gods, which is why Socrates in the Philebus fears to misuse divine names.","ideas":["Divine generation in myth is symbolic of causal procession","Paternal cause is bound and monad; maternal is duad and infinity","Names exist at three levels, with human names as statues of the Gods"],"terms":[{"t":"peras and apeiron (bound and infinite)","d":"The two principles that fathers and mothers in myth represent."}]},{"i":342,"j":350,"loc":"Book II, Chapter I (1 of 2)","title":"If only the many existed, without the one","summary":"Book II opens the search for the first cause with three options: beings are many only, one only, or both one and many. Proclus first refutes the many-without-one. Each of the many must be some one thing or nothing; without the one, every part would be infinitely infinite, which is impossible and unknowable. Such a many would also be both similar and dissimilar, same and different, moved and unmoved by the same privation of unity. Nor would there be number, whose principle is the one, nor speech or knowledge, which require union of knower and known.","ideas":["Every being is either one something or nothing","A many without the one would be infinitely infinite","Contradictory attributes follow from total lack of unity","Number and knowledge both depend on the one"],"terms":[]},{"i":351,"j":360,"loc":"Book II, Chapter I (2 of 2)","title":"If only the one existed; the one beyond multitude","summary":"Proclus next refutes the view that the one alone exists: there would be no whole or parts, no beginning or end, no figure, nothing in itself or in another, no motion or rest, no sameness, difference, likeness, contact or equality. Since both extremes fail, being must be both one and many. He then asks how they relate: if neither participates the other, the absurdities of the many-only return; if they mix, some third cause of the mixture must exist, which leads either to regress or to a one free of multitude. He concludes that the many participate the one, that the one is unmixed with multitude, and that nothing is better than the one, which causes the being of the many.","ideas":["A sheer one excludes all predicates, so it cannot be all that exists","Being must be both one and many","The mixture of one and many needs a cause prior to both","The imparticipable one is beyond and causes multitude"],"terms":[{"t":"imparticipable one","d":"The first one, unmixed with the many, distinct from the one present in things."}]},{"i":361,"j":374,"loc":"Book II, Chapter II","title":"The first principle is beyond essence","summary":"A second argument divides the options: one principle or many; if one, essence or not; if essence, corporeal or not, separable or not, moved or unmoved. Many unrelated principles produce nothing common, related ones need something common prior to them, and a finite number of principles requires one as their cause. Body cannot be the principle, since it is divisible and needs an incorporeal bond and an infinite, incorporeal power to persist. An inseparable principle could not produce intellect, and a moved one would desire something beyond itself, so the principle is immoveable. Proclus then shows the one is prior to essence and that the principle cannot be participated by essence, so the cause of all is above every essence.","ideas":["Many principles require one prior to them","The principle is incorporeal, separable and immoveable","The one and essence differ, and the one is prior","The first cause is neither essence nor has essence added to it"],"terms":[{"t":"ousia (essence)","d":"Being or substance, which Proclus places below the one."}]},{"i":375,"j":384,"loc":"Book II, Chapter III","title":"The one present in all; no infinite regress","summary":"A third argument asks what all beings participate. Not life or motion, rest, intellect, or even essence, since things coming to be lack essence; only the one is present to everything, since what lacks the one is nothing, and this pervasive one comes from the first one. A further argument rules out an infinite series of causes in either direction, which would make things infinitely infinite, deprive them of conversion to their principle, and leave no common object of desire or standard of better and worse. Causes cannot come in a circle, nor can the perfect come from the imperfect. So the principle is one, the last of beings is also one, and the one is exempt from all multitude.","ideas":["Unity is the only feature shared by all that exists","Infinite causal series destroy knowledge and conversion","The better cannot arise from the worse","The unmixed precedes the mixed in every genus"],"terms":[]},{"i":385,"j":395,"loc":"Book II, Chapter IV","title":"Against Origen: the one beyond intellect","summary":"Proclus wonders at interpreters who accept the intellectual realm but not the one's transcendence, especially Origen, fellow pupil of Plotinus, who stopped at intellect and first being. If Origen passed over the one as beyond speech that agrees with Plato, but not if he thought it nonexistent; the latter Proclus calls a Peripatetic innovation. He cites the Republic's sun analogy, where the good is beyond essence and its light of truth makes intellect divine; the Sophista, where the one being participates the one; the Philebus, where intellect alone is not the human good; and the Parmenides, where the one is denied essence, rest, motion and being in itself.","ideas":["Origen ends at intellect, omitting the one","The good is beyond essence in dignity and power (Republic)","Being is one by participation, so the one is prior (Sophista)","Intellect is insufficient as the good (Philebus)"],"terms":[{"t":"light of truth","d":"The light from the good uniting intellect and intelligible, as sunlight joins eye and visible."}]},{"i":396,"j":406,"loc":"Book II, Chapters V–VI","title":"Two ways to the first: analogy and negation","summary":"Since the one is neither intelligible nor intellectual, Proclus asks how Plato leads toward it, and finds two modes: analogy, as with the sun in the Republic, and negation, as in the Parmenides. Negation reflects the procession of all from the first, analogy their conversion back to it; and he distinguishes negations that are above, equal to, or below affirmations. The first has no relation to what follows and transcends all equally. Correspondingly there are two names: the one, imaging procession and the source of multitude, and the good, imaging conversion and object of all desire; yet the ineffable is not truly named, and the union of all things with it remains unknown.","ideas":["Negation shows procession; analogy shows conversion","Negations can be superior, coordinate or privative","The one and the good are names taken from effects","All things abide in, proceed from and return to the one"],"terms":[{"t":"apophasis (negation)","d":"Denial that can express transcendence rather than mere privation."}]},{"i":407,"j":418,"loc":"Book II, Chapter VII","title":"The Republic's sun and the superessential good","summary":"Proclus reads Republic VI: Socrates sets one monad over intelligibles and the sun over sensibles, making the sun the good's offspring, so that all things are suspended from the good. This models theology: mundane Gods are referred to supermundane ones, these to intellectual, these to intelligible, and those to the first. The good itself must not be confused with the form of good among intelligibles; it is beyond knowledge and essence, and the denials that it is truth, essence or intellect epitomize the Parmenides' negations. The good generates all things by its fullness, not by motion, multiplication, power or energy, all of which are secondary to it, and it is the end of all ends.","ideas":["Each order has a monad analogous to the good, like the sun","The superessential good differs from the good as a form","The good produces all without motion, power or energy","The good is the final end and centre of all desire"],"terms":[{"t":"monad","d":"The leading unity of a series, to which its members are referred."}]},{"i":419,"j":424,"loc":"Book II, Chapter VIII","title":"The king of all in the Second Letter","summary":"Proclus turns to Plato's letter to Dionysius, where all things are said to be about the king of all, for his sake, and he the cause of all beautiful things. He answers an objector who thinks the three kings are intellectual Gods and that the good cannot be counted with lesser natures: Plato calls only the first God king, gives him no share of number, and makes him lord of all things alike rather than leader of a triad. The analogy of the sun in the Republic, he adds, does not rank the good alongside its images. Plato's remark that the inquiry treats the cause as something with a quality shows that the first principle cannot be spoken or known. The soul, and every divine nature, reaches it only by leaving its own multiplicity behind and uniting through the ineffable symbol of the father planted in all things.","ideas":["The first God alone is called king, and is king of all things, not first of three","Being exempt from all, he is not coordinated with secondary causes","Speech about him speaks of his effects, never of him","All things carry an ineffable impression of the first through which they are united to him"],"terms":[{"t":"exempt transcendency","d":"The first cause's standing wholly above its effects, not as one member of a series with them."},{"t":"hyparxis","d":"The highest, unitary mode of a being's existence, through which it touches the one."}]},{"i":425,"j":429,"loc":"Book II, Chapter IX","title":"Three ranked ways of speaking the first","summary":"Proclus ranks the three clauses of the letter as three conceptions of the first principle. The best is that all things are about him, which says nothing of him and leaves him unconnected with what follows; second is that all things are for his sake, which names him the common end of all desire; third, and furthest from his union, is that he is cause of all beautiful things, which adds productive causality. Strictly the first should be honoured in silence, though the soul's longing to speak of him is pardoned. He ends by locating the 'about which', the 'on account of which' and the 'from which' in the summit, middle and extremity of the intelligible Gods, from whom these titles are transferred to the first.","ideas":["'About him' is the purest way to indicate the first, since it adds nothing to him","Final cause stands nearest to what is beyond cause; productive cause is more remote","The one and the good are named from the gifts that reach all things from him","The three prepositions mark the three ranks of intelligibles"],"terms":[{"t":"final cause","d":"That for the sake of which all things exist, here the good as the object of all desire."}]},{"i":430,"j":435,"loc":"Book II, Chapters X–XI","title":"Rules for the negations; a hymn of ascent","summary":"Proclus turns to the first hypothesis of the Parmenides, which denies of the one as many attributes as the second hypothesis affirms, showing the one exempt from every divine genus. He lays down three rules: the negations follow the order of the divine ranks, starting where multitude first appears and returning at the end to the Gods nearest the one; they do not deprive but generate their opposites; and in the end even negation must be removed, since no discourse or name belongs to the one. He then calls for a stilling of opinion, imagination and passion so that the soul, as if with closed eyes before the rising sun, may hymn the first God as God of all Gods and unity of unities. From that hymn the discussion descends again to dialectic.","ideas":["Causes exempt from their effects produce more perfectly than coordinate ones","The negations proceed in the order of the divine processions","Negation here is generative, not privative","Discourse about the ineffable ends by overturning itself"],"terms":[{"t":"first hypothesis","d":"The opening argument of the Parmenides' second part, read by Proclus as the negative theology of the one."},{"t":"anagogic","d":"Leading the soul upward toward the first principle."}]},{"i":436,"j":445,"loc":"Book II, Chapter XII","title":"Negations mapped onto the divine orders","summary":"Proclus follows the conclusions of the first hypothesis one by one, pairing each denial with an order of Gods that the one produces while staying above it. Not many, not a whole with parts, without beginning, middle, end or figure answer to the intelligible-and-intellectual ranks; neither in itself nor in another, neither moving nor at rest, neither same nor different answer to the intellectual Gods. Similar and dissimilar match the supermundane Gods, equal and unequal the celestial and sublunary mundane Gods, and older and younger the divine souls that live by time. Parmenides then returns to the top, denying of the one even participation in essence, and closes with the ineffable, so that every order hangs from a one that shares in none of them.","ideas":["Each negation names an order the one gives rise to by transcending it","One, two and three negations fall to the three intelligible-intellectual ranks","Time and its divisions belong to divine souls, not to the one","The final denial of essence is reached by intellect, not drawn from earlier steps"],"terms":[{"t":"intellectual projection","d":"A direct act of intellect, as opposed to step-by-step logical inference."}]},{"i":446,"j":452,"loc":"Book III, Chapter I (part)","title":"Axioms of procession by similitude","summary":"Opening Book III, Proclus moves from the first God to the many Gods and first sets out axioms to show that there are as many divine orders as the second hypothesis of the Parmenides lists. Every procession comes about through similitude, since an effect wholly the same as its cause or wholly different from it could not proceed from or turn back to it. Each monad produces a number of its own kind before dissimilar ones, as nature, the mathematical monad and soul show. Effects nearer their cause extend further and produce more, and lesser production means lesser power and essence. Finally, imparticipable causes always precede participated ones, as one soul and one intellect precede the many souls and intellects that share in them.","ideas":["Without similitude there is neither generation nor return","Every monad produces a multitude like itself","Greater productive range marks nearness to the first","Imparticipable monads precede participated ones at every level"],"terms":[{"t":"monad","d":"A unitary first term that holds in itself, undivided, what its series unfolds separately."},{"t":"imparticipable","d":"Existing in itself and not shared by any lower being, as opposed to participated."}]},{"i":453,"j":463,"loc":"Book III, Chapters I–II","title":"The Gods as participated superessential unities","summary":"From these axioms Proclus concludes that the one produces first a multitude of self-perfect unities, the Gods, which are most like it and through which beings are joined to it. He asks whether these unities are imparticipable like the one and answers that they are participated by beings, citing the second hypothesis, where the one and being share in each other. Socrates' image in the Republic of light proceeding from the good is read the same way: that light is the participated deity which makes intelligibles one. The unities form an order like numbers: those nearer the one are shared by simpler essences, those further away by more composite ones, as the gradation of celestial, elemental and partial souls and of intellects illustrates.","ideas":["After the one come many Gods, superessential and most like it","The Gods are unities participated by beings, unlike the one itself","Light from the good in the Republic is the participated divine unity","Higher unities are shared by simpler beings, lower ones by more composite"],"terms":[{"t":"henad / unity","d":"One of the many superessential Gods that come after the one and are participated by beings."},{"t":"superessential","d":"Above essence or being."}]},{"i":464,"j":475,"loc":"Book III, Chapter II","title":"The ladder of being, life, intellect, soul","summary":"Proclus orders the kinds of beings that the unities are participated by. Body cannot cause its own being and gets its perfection from soul; soul shares in intellect; life, which even plants have, ranks above intellect; and being, shared by things without life, stands above life, since the cause of more effects is nearer the one. He supports the order from the Republic, Phaedrus, Timaeus, Philebus, Laws and above all the Sophist, where perfect being is said to contain life, intellect and soul. Each higher cause works on everything below it, so soul produces in four ways and being reaches all things. The unities are then matched to these ranks, the highest deifying being itself, which is why Parmenides begins from the one being.","ideas":["Being is wider than life, life than intellect, intellect than soul","The cause of more effects stands nearer the one","Soul produces in a fourfold way, being reaches even privations","Different ranks of Gods deify being, life, intellect, soul and body"],"terms":[{"t":"gnostic","d":"Having some power of knowledge, which Proclus traces to intellect."}]},{"i":476,"j":486,"loc":"Book III, Chapter III","title":"Bound and infinity as the two principles","summary":"Proclus restates the one as the cause named the good in the Republic, the one in the Parmenides, the king in the Letters and the cause of deity in the Philebus, and honours it in silence. After it come two principles which the Philebus calls bound and infinity, from whose mixture God produces all beings. Because they mix in beings, they must first exist unmixed before beings. Bound is the cause of stable, unifying, measuring deity; infinity of power, multiplication and generative progression. He traces the pair through eternity, intellect, soul, the heavens, generation and finally form and matter in natural things, which are their lowest images.","ideas":["Two principles follow the one: bound and infinity","They exist unmixed before they are mixed in beings","Remaining belongs to bound, proceeding to infinity","Form and matter are the last reflections of the pair"],"terms":[{"t":"peras (bound)","d":"The principle of limit, unity and measure that comes directly after the one."},{"t":"apeiron (infinity)","d":"The principle of unlimited generative power and multiplication paired with bound."}]},{"i":487,"j":494,"loc":"Book III, Chapter IX","title":"The mixed as first being","summary":"The third thing after the two principles is what the Philebus calls the mixed, and Proclus identifies it with the first being or essence, which holds essence, life and intellect intelligibly. The two principles are said to be shown forth by God, while the mixture is made, a lower mode of coming forth; and the cause of the mixture, the fourth thing in the Philebus, must be the first God himself, as the Republic's account of the good as cause of being also suggests. The mixed is a monad, a duad and a triad. He explains how essence can come from principles above essence, as a compound differs from its ingredients, and reads the Sophist's definition of being as power in this light. Plotinus is right to pair form with hyparxis, but intelligible matter must not be thought formless: infinity is the power of bound, not its matter.","ideas":["The first mixture of bound and infinity is being itself","The first God is the cause of the mixture","Being is a one-many: hyparxis from bound, power from infinity","Infinity is the power of bound, not formless matter"],"terms":[{"t":"the mixed","d":"In the Philebus the product of bound and infinity; for Proclus the first intelligible essence."},{"t":"ousia (essence)","d":"Being in its first and highest form, the summit of all beings."}]},{"i":495,"j":504,"loc":"Book III, Chapters X–XII","title":"Twofold principles; symmetry, truth, beauty","summary":"Generation is also a mixture of bound and infinity, but there infinity is imperfect power, that is, matter, so that lower things imitate the higher by deficiency. Proclus distinguishes two kinds of bound and infinity, one prior to and producing the mixture, the other present in it as an element, which settles how the Philebus can set the mixed before them. He then takes up the triad present in every right mixture, symmetry, truth and beauty: symmetry makes being one, truth makes it real, beauty makes it intelligible, and Iamblichus defined the intelligible by these three. They stand in the vestibules of the good. He sums up the first intelligible triad: bound as measuring God, infinity as its unfailing power, and the mixed as the highest order of Gods.","ideas":["Matter is the lowest, imperfect image of the power in being","Bound and infinity exist both before the mixture and within it","Symmetry, truth and beauty belong to every right mixture","The first intelligible triad: bound, infinity, mixed"],"terms":[{"t":"vestibules of the good","d":"The Philebus image for the triad of symmetry, truth and beauty, standing just before the good."}]},{"i":505,"j":515,"loc":"Book III, Chapters XIII–XIV","title":"Second and third intelligible triads","summary":"Proclus argues that a second triad follows the first in the same pattern: a unity or hyparxis, a power, and a secondary being, which is intelligible life, since being that proceeds implies motion and so life. It is marked by infinity as the first is by bound, and chiefly by truth. A third triad then constitutes intelligible intellect, in which all intelligible multitude and the forms appear openly; it is marked by the mixed and chiefly by beauty. The three triads stand to one another as remaining, proceeding and returning, and together reflect the first God's union, his power and his generation of beings. Being itself is cause but not paradigm; forms, which separate, first appear in the third triad.","ideas":["Each intelligible triad has bound, infinity and the mixed","Essence, life and intellect are the mixtures of the three triads","Symmetry, truth and beauty predominate in the first, second and third","Forms first appear at the extremity of the intelligible order"],"terms":[{"t":"intelligible intellect","d":"The third intelligible triad, where intelligible multitude and the first forms appear."}]},{"i":516,"j":523,"loc":"Book III, Chapters XV–XVII","title":"Animal itself, eternity and the one","summary":"Following Syrianus, Proclus seeks the triads in the Timaeus. The paradigm of the world, animal itself, is all-perfect, only-begotten and most beautiful, which places it among the intelligibles and above the demiurge's intellectual paradigm; since it contains separated forms it belongs to the third triad. Because it is eternal it participates in eternity, which must be prior to it. Eternity, the source of immortality and so a kind of life, sits in the middle triad, below being, which neither rests nor moves, and above intelligible intellect. The one in which Timaeus says eternity abides cannot be the imparticipable one or eternity itself, so it is the first triad.","ideas":["Animal itself is the paradigm and belongs to the third intelligible triad","Eternity is prior to animal itself and is life, the middle triad","Eternity abides in the first triad, which Timaeus calls the one","The demiurge's intelligible content is intellectual, below animal itself"],"terms":[{"t":"autozōon (animal itself)","d":"The Timaeus' intelligible model containing all intelligible animals."},{"t":"aiōn (eternity)","d":"The intelligible measure of perpetual being, the model of time."}]},{"i":524,"j":533,"loc":"Book III, Chapter XVIII","title":"The Timaean triads and the most beautiful","summary":"Proclus sets the three Timaean names, the one, eternity and animal itself, against the three triads and explains why each is so called. Eternity holds bound and infinity together, as time shows them divided in the now and in continuity, and Plotinus rightly called it infinite life. Animal itself is only-begotten through bound, eternal through power and all-perfect through intellect, and the Timaeus recalls these in its accounts of the universe's oneness, time and completeness. It is most beautiful because beauty shines forth in the third progression, as truth does in the second and symmetry in the first. Beauty, the form of forms, astonishes and turns the soul, as the mysteries do before their final sights.","ideas":["One, eternity and animal itself name the three triads","Time reflects eternity's bound and infinity in divided form","Only-begotten, eternal, all-perfect match bound, infinity, mixed","Beauty first appears at the third triad and draws the soul upward"],"terms":[{"t":"only-begotten","d":"Title of the paradigm as unique and whole, not paired with another."}]},{"i":534,"j":549,"loc":"Book III, Chapters XIX–XX","title":"Four ideas; Sophist's one being, whole, all","summary":"Since forms appear in the intelligible, the genera of being must be there too, hidden: essence in the first triad, motion and rest as life in the second, sameness and difference in the third. Intelligible intellect divides into a monad and triad, the four Timaean ideas: the celestial Gods, and the winged, aquatic and terrestrial kinds matched to bound, infinity and the mixed. Proclus then reads the Sophist's arguments against Parmenides' 'all is one': the one is not the one being, since the names would coincide; not the whole, since a whole has parts; and not the all, which is divided into many. These three, one being, whole and all, belong to the first, second and third triads.","ideas":["The genera of being exist in the intelligible triads","Four primary ideas: celestial, winged, aquatic, terrestrial","The Eleatic Stranger separates the one from one being, whole and all","The demiurge models the cosmos on the all and time on the whole"],"terms":[{"t":"one being","d":"The one as it participates in being; for Proclus the first intelligible triad."}]},{"i":550,"j":559,"loc":"Book III, Chapters XXI–XXII","title":"Father, power, intellect; good, wise, beautiful","summary":"In each triad bound is called father, infinity power and the mixed intellect, and Proclus defends these names as Plato's, distinguishing three senses of intellect. He finds the triad most plainly in the Timaeus demiurge, who calls himself father, speaks of his power to generate and is called intellect, and holds these on account of the intelligibles. Being, eternity and intellect differ in extent, being reaching even matter. From the Phaedrus he takes the good, the wise and the beautiful for the three triads, with their elements: desirable, sufficient and perfect; full, prolific and convertive; and lovely, delicate and splendid. Splendid beauty at the extremity astonished the intellectual Gods, as Orpheus says.","ideas":["Each triad is father, power and intellect","The demiurge shows this triad in divided form","Good, wise and beautiful characterize the three triads","Each quality has three elements matched to bound, infinity, mixed"],"terms":[{"t":"demiurge","d":"The maker of the cosmos in the Timaeus, here an intellectual God reflecting the intelligible triad."}]},{"i":560,"j":565,"loc":"Book III, Chapters XXIII–XXIV","title":"Second hypothesis: the first triad","summary":"Proclus turns to the Parmenides, recalling that the second hypothesis traces the procession of the Gods, while the first concerns the first God alone. He rejects readers who take the first hypothesis to be about the Gods in general or about the intelligible Gods, since the same items are affirmed in the second. Following Syrianus, he will match conclusions to orders in sequence. The first intelligible triad is the one, power and being, with power as the link through which the one is shared by being. Parmenides gives it at the start of the second hypothesis, where the one is said to participate in essence; the Gods' powers are superessential.","ideas":["The first hypothesis is about the first God, the second about the Gods","Conclusions are matched to the divine orders in sequence","First triad: one, power, being, with power hidden between","Divine powers are above essence and can do all things"],"terms":[{"t":"second hypothesis","d":"The argument 'if the one is', read by Proclus as the procession of all divine orders."}]},{"i":566,"j":572,"loc":"Book III, Chapters XXV–XXVI","title":"Second hypothesis: wholeness and infinite multitude","summary":"The second triad, intelligible wholeness, appears in Parmenides' conclusion that the one being is a whole with parts, the one and being, held apart and together by power. Proclus distinguishes three wholenesses, before the parts, from the parts and in a part, all held in this triad. The third triad appears where each part again contains the one and being, giving endless division into an infinite multitude. Parmenides first speaks of generation here, marking this triad as the first to generate. The infinity in question is not one of quantity but of power, the offspring of the first infinity, which includes all multitude and so is all-perfect.","ideas":["The second triad is the whole with two parts, one and being","Wholeness is threefold: before, from and in parts","Repeated division of one and being yields intelligible multitude","Intelligible infinity is infinite power, not quantity"],"terms":[{"t":"intelligible wholeness","d":"The second intelligible triad, where the one and being first appear as distinct parts of a whole."}]},{"i":573,"j":585,"loc":"Book III, Chapters XXVII–XXVIII","title":"The intelligible triads drawn together","summary":"Proclus gathers Plato's names for each triad: the first is the one, the one being, or bound, infinity and the mixed; the second is eternity and the first wholeness, which agree because whatever is eternal is whole; the third is animal itself, infinite multitude and the intelligible divided into many. He solves a puzzle about how the same four ideas make Gods, daemons and mortals: each form leads a series down to the last things, and the parts of the one being, some marked by the one, some by being, produce Gods, angels, daemons and mortals. He ends by setting the whole intelligible genus above all other orders, as the one is above all Gods.","ideas":["Each triad bears several names across the Timaeus, Sophist and Parmenides","Eternity and wholeness coincide","One form produces Gods, daemons and mortals at different levels","The intelligible Gods give intelligible fullness to every later order"],"terms":[{"t":"daemons and angels","d":"Kinds of being ranked between the Gods and mortals, here derived from parts of intelligible multitude."}]},{"i":586,"j":594,"loc":"Book IV, Chapter I","title":"Introducing the intelligible-and-intellectual Gods","summary":"Opening Book IV, Proclus turns from the intelligible to the intellectual Gods, starting with those that are both intelligible and intellectual. Recalling the monads of essence, life and intellect, he places these Gods at the level of life, between intelligible being and imparticipable intellect. They are filled from the intelligible and fill the intellectual Gods, perceiving those above them and perceived by those below, and so take their name from both. They proceed in triads with more separation and variety than the intelligibles, and the partial orders beneath them are beyond our counting. The Timaeus' 'most beautiful of intelligibles' implies intelligibles below animal itself, which are these Gods.","ideas":["The intelligible-intellectual Gods stand at the level of life","They connect the intelligible and the intellectual orders","Distance from the one brings more division and less power","Animal itself is the boundary of the first intelligibles"],"terms":[{"t":"intelligible and intellectual Gods","d":"The middle rank of Gods, objects of thought to those below and thinkers of those above."}]},{"i":595,"j":604,"loc":"Book IV, Chapters II–III","title":"How the intelligible-intellectual Gods proceed and divide","summary":"Proclus explains how the Gods who illuminate the breadth of imparticipable life proceed from the intelligible Gods, unfolding their hidden union into multitude. Life exists causally in intelligibles, essentially in this middle order, and by participation in intellectuals; the three intelligible powers (essence-making, measuring, form-producing) have counterparts here. The order is divided triply: its summit joins the intelligibles, its end the intellectuals, and its middle holds the bond of both; essence, life and intellect are all present in it vitally. Proclus concludes there are three intelligible-intellectual triads, each containing essence, life and intellect in its own mode, unlike the intelligible triads, each of which held only a third part of being.","ideas":["Divine orders proceed through continuous likeness: the end of a higher order resembles the start of the next","The middle order receives progression from intelligible power and its being from intelligible life","Three triads arise, imitating abiding, procession and conversion","Each new triad has essence, life and intellect, whereas each intelligible triad held bound, infinity and the mixed"],"terms":[{"t":"intelligible and at the same time intellectual Gods","d":"The middle order of Gods, perceiving those above them and perceived by those below."},{"t":"hyparxis","d":"The primary, characteristic existence or summit of a divine nature."}]},{"i":605,"j":614,"loc":"Book IV, Chapters IV–VI","title":"The Phædrus ascent and the intelligible heaven","summary":"Proclus turns to Plato and recalls the Phædrus: Jupiter leads twelve Gods and the dæmons to the subcelestial arch, the heaven, its back, and the supercelestial place with its plain of truth, Adrastia and the choir of virtues. He argues the heaven cannot be the sensible heaven, since the Gods would then turn toward inferior things and souls would be moved by bodies rather than moving them. With Plotinus, Iamblichus and the Cratylus etymology of Heaven as sight looking upward, he places Heaven between the intelligible and intellectual orders. The supercelestial place, which holds a multitude of forms and nourishes souls, cannot be the first intelligibles; it is the intelligible summit of the intellectual Gods.","ideas":["The Gods know sensibles through containing their causes, not by turning to them","Heaven is intelligence, the medium between intellect and the intelligible","The supercelestial place is below the first intelligible triads but above the heaven","Intelligibles nourish the wing of the soul"],"terms":[{"t":"supercelestial place","d":"The Phædrus region beyond the heaven, taken by Proclus as the first intelligible-intellectual triad."},{"t":"Ouranos (Heaven)","d":"In the Cratylus, sight looking upward; for Proclus the connective middle order of Gods."}]},{"i":615,"j":625,"loc":"Book IV, Chapters VII–IX","title":"The subcelestial arch and the stages of ascent","summary":"The subcelestial arch lies under and within the heaven, not at the summit of the intellectuals; through it the Gods are converted and raised to the celestial circulation and filled from the supercelestial place. Proclus explains that the whole order is named from its middle because it binds the extremes, the one above, the other below. He then praises Plato for describing an ascent like that of the highest initiators: through liberated, perfective and connective Gods up to the intelligible. Reading the Phædrus on the mysteries, he links initiation, entire visions and epopteia to these orders, and names love, truth and faith as the three elevating causes.","ideas":["Supercelestial place gives the intelligible, the heaven intelligence, the arch conversion","The order is named from its middle, which binds the two extremes","Plato's ascent of souls mirrors theurgic ascent through intermediate Gods","Faith, beyond all knowing, establishes souls in the ineffable"],"terms":[{"t":"subcelestial arch","d":"The order directly under the heaven; for Proclus the perfective triad."},{"t":"muesis and epopteia","d":"Initiation and final beholding in the mysteries, read as symbols of union with intelligibles."}]},{"i":626,"j":637,"loc":"Book IV, Chapters X–XI","title":"The supercelestial place: intelligible, maternal, described negatively","summary":"Proclus argues the supercelestial place is intelligible, yet not simply so: it is intelligible as the summit of intellectuals, as Saturn is intelligible relative to the demiurgus. Unlike the paternal first intelligible triad, it is maternal and prolific, a place that receives paternal causes and generates within itself, which is why Plato calls it a place and its contents are feminine. He then asks why Plato speaks of it negatively, as without colour, figure and contact. Because it is the highest of its order, it imitates the unknown first principle and is praised negatively; because it participates in the first intelligibles, it is also praised affirmatively, as essence that truly is and the plain of truth.","ideas":["Plato calls it truly-existing essence, not the one being","It is a feminine, generative place that keeps its offspring within itself","The summit of every order is analogous to the one and celebrated by negations","Both negation and affirmation suit an order that mediates"],"terms":[{"t":"intelligible of intellectuals","d":"An intelligible nature existing at the top of the intellectual order."}]},{"i":638,"j":648,"loc":"Book IV, Chapters XII–XIV","title":"Colour, figure, contact; and the three virtues","summary":"Proclus explains that the negations of the Phædrus deny of the supercelestial place the orders right below it: colour is the heaven, figure the subcelestial arch, contact the summit of the properly intellectual Gods, as the Parmenides suggests. The place transcends these causally, not by privation, so readings about sensible colours and shapes are dismissed as fit for physiologists. The affirmation that it is truly existing essence shows it participates in the first being. Proclus then treats science, temperance and justice as intelligible goddesses and fountains of virtue, not mere forms, assigning to them knowledge of higher things, conversion to oneself, and distribution according to desert.","ideas":["Each negation denies a lower divine order","The place exceeds colour, figure and contact as their cause","Every intelligible truly is, and every true being is intelligible","Science, temperance and justice parallel the three intelligible triads"],"terms":[{"t":"Synoches / connective Gods","d":"The Gods of the heaven, whom the negation of colour denies of the place above them."}]},{"i":649,"j":663,"loc":"Book IV, Chapters XV–XVIII","title":"Plain, meadow, nectar, ambrosia, and Adrastia","summary":"A further triad belongs to the supercelestial place: the plain of truth, expanded to intelligible light; the meadow, prolific of life and forms; and the nutriment of the Gods. Ambrosia, solid and akin to bound, gives stability; nectar, liquid and akin to infinity, gives unfailing providence. Proclus, following Syrianus and the Orphic theology, divides the place into three monads, aligning them with science, temperance and justice and with abiding, procession and return. Adrastia rules here, binding the Saturnian, Jovian and fatal laws under one sacred law (thesmos) that nothing escapes. A summary closes the treatment of the place and urges the soul to approach it, leaving the body behind.","ideas":["Ambrosia gives establishment in higher natures; nectar gives providence for lower ones","The banquet stands for divided distribution, the feast for united conversion","Adrastia's thesmos guards every divine law","Orpheus pictures Adrastia sounding a drum to turn the Gods toward her"],"terms":[{"t":"Adrastia","d":"The inescapable goddess whose sacred law gives each soul its measure of blessedness."},{"t":"thesmos and nomos","d":"Sacred law, nearer the intelligible, compared with law fitted to intellectual distribution."}]},{"i":664,"j":670,"loc":"Book IV, Chapters XIX–XX","title":"What connects all things: the celestial circulation","summary":"Proclus asks what first connects things and rejects candidates in turn: body and spirit scatter; material habits and forms cannot bind themselves; souls are mixed and stretched in time; intellect is already divided and needs binding. The connective order therefore stands before the intellectual Gods. Three triads, collective, connective and perfective, gather, hold together and complete every multitude, imitating the unific intelligible triads. The connective triad is the Phædrus celestial circulation: eternal, unchanging intelligence about which souls revolve in time. As the visible heaven presses the elements together and leaves no void, the intellectual heaven binds intellect, soul and body each to its degree.","ideas":["Whatever is divisible needs a higher cause to connect it","Collective, connective and perfective triads complete multitude","Souls circle in time; the celestial circulation stays the same","The visible heaven's binding of the elements images the divine one"],"terms":[{"t":"synochē (connexion)","d":"Gathering many into indivisible communion, the property of the middle triad."}]},{"i":671,"j":681,"loc":"Book IV, Chapters XXI–XXII","title":"Back, depth, and arch of the heaven","summary":"Proclus divides the heaven into three: its back, an intelligible deity on which souls stand while the circulation carries them round; its profundity, the middle, life-giving continuity; and the celestial arch, its intellectual lower limit, distinct from the subcelestial arch. The heaven connects itself first, so it is one and continuous yet triple, like the smooth outer surface Timæus gives the visible heaven. From the Cratylus he shows the middle position: Heaven sees the things above and generates a pure intellect. The first monad mostly perceives intelligibles, the third generates intellectuals, and the middle shares in both; purity in the intellect it produces points to the Gods of undefiled power.","ideas":["Back is intelligible, profundity is life, arch is intellect","Souls standing on the back combine rest and motion","Heaven is intelligence to the intelligibles and intelligible to the intellectuals","Orphic theologists make Heaven, father of Saturn, the third"],"terms":[{"t":"Synocheus","d":"One of the connective Gods; the first is bound up with the supercelestial place."}]},{"i":682,"j":692,"loc":"Book IV, Chapters XXIII–XXV","title":"The perfective triad of the subcelestial arch","summary":"Proclus reviews earlier views: Theodorus of Asine, following Plotinus, made the first God Heaven and the subcelestial arch what follows it, while Iamblichus placed Heaven among the intelligibles; Syrianus is credited with settling the order. All of them distinguish the arch from the heaven. The arch ends the middle progression and converts it to its source; Proclus rejects locating first perfection in entelechies, soul or intellect. Since souls are initiated there, its Gods may be called Teletarchs. Perfection is triple: before parts, from parts, and in a part, also stable, generative and convertive, assigned to three leaders under the three connective Gods.","ideas":["Theodorus, Iamblichus and Syrianus all distinguish arch from heaven","Perfection separate from things must precede perfection in time or in intellect","The Teletarchs initiate souls in the most blessed mysteries","Perfection is threefold, like wholeness"],"terms":[{"t":"Teletarchs","d":"Leaders of initiation (teletē), the perfective Gods of the subcelestial arch."}]},{"i":693,"j":700,"loc":"Book IV, Chapters XXVI–XXVIII","title":"Mysteries of ascent and Parmenidean number","summary":"Proclus closes the Phædrus account: telete precedes muesis, which precedes epopteia, and he prays that the Teletarchs fill readers with intelligible beauty. He then turns to the second hypothesis of the Parmenides, where three intelligible-intellectual triads, number, whole and the perfect, match the intelligible triads of the one being, whole and infinite multitude. Difference, which was power in intelligibles, first appears here as a maternal fountain that separates the one from being and multiplies both into number. So intellectual number is separated multitude, while intelligible multitude stays united in the monad and duad, paternally and maternally.","ideas":["Teletarchs initiate; connective Gods give entire visions; collective Gods give the beholding","Number, whole and the perfect correspond to the intelligible triads","Difference breaks being into beings and the one into unities","In intelligibles multitude exists causally; here number exists by participation"],"terms":[{"t":"telete, muesis, epopteia","d":"Stages of the mysteries matched to the perfective, connective and collective Gods."}]},{"i":701,"j":707,"loc":"Book IV, Chapter XXIX","title":"Powers of intellectual number: odd and even","summary":"Intellectual number has two powers: through the monad and the odd it gathers multitude into union; through the duad and the even it produces multitude and divides it. Through these it forms coordinations, better and worse, male and female, down to matter. Number precedes intellectuals and produces intellectual, supermundane, celestial and sublunary numbers; the Athenian guest's rule of odd offerings to celestial and even to terrestrial powers is cited. The perfect number of the Muses, Phædrus periods and number in Timæus' time show that divine number orders all. Proclus adds that numbers even precede forms, since there are superessential numbers but not superessential forms.","ideas":["Odd governs the simple and unified; even the varied and generative","Number exists before what is numbered","Every form is number, as the Pythagoreans held","Timæus' four intelligible ideas show number in the monad"],"terms":[{"t":"superessential numbers","d":"Numbers above being, belonging to the Gods themselves rather than to forms."}]},{"i":708,"j":717,"loc":"Book IV, Chapters XXX–XXXI","title":"Feminine difference and the threefold first number","summary":"Proclus argues the first number is feminine because difference first shows itself there, and this difference is not the genus 'different' among beings: it lies between the one and being, belongs to unities, and stands before the demiurgic same and different. He calls it the feminine nature of the Gods, imitating intelligible power but dividing rather than joining. Male is to bound what female is to infinity. Following Parmenides, he derives three monads and three duads from the one, difference and being, and from them, the oddly-odd, evenly-even and evenly-odd. The third monad unfolds all number, and the infinity it reaches is not one of quantity.","ideas":["Generative difference differs from the genus of being called the different","Power exists before, in and after male and female","Once, twice, thrice are the powers of monad, duad, triad","The infinite always stands at the end of a triad"],"terms":[{"t":"oddly-odd, evenly-even, evenly-odd","d":"Number kinds that collect, generate and mix, derived from the first triad."}]},{"i":718,"j":730,"loc":"Book IV, Chapters XXXII–XXXIV","title":"Number after animal itself, and its unknown power","summary":"Proclus takes up Plotinus' claim that number comes before animal itself: right if it means hidden number held in a monad, wrong if it means divided number, which belongs to intellectuals. Animal itself is the only-begotten tetrad, monad and triad. He then cites the Parmenides passages for the three monads and shows how Plato's order follows them. Divine numbers are said to be unknown and infinite to partial minds, are used in the highest sacred works, and carry an anagogic power to draw souls toward truth. Timæus' time, which proceeds by number, and the Muses' perfect number show that the first number is generative, measuring and perfective.","ideas":["Multitude belongs to the end of the intelligibles; number to the start of the intellectuals","Plato moves through monad to triad, even numbers, then being back to the one","Ultimate numbers carry an unknown quality from intelligible numbers","Number restores periods and governs good and bad births"],"terms":[{"t":"anagogic","d":"Leading upward, from sensible things toward intelligibles."}]},{"i":731,"j":739,"loc":"Book IV, Chapters XXXV–XXXVII","title":"Connective and perfective triads in the Parmenides","summary":"The middle, connective triad appears in the Parmenides as one and many, whole and parts, finite and infinite, its three monads intelligible, intelligible-intellectual and intellectual. The one is beyond whole and whole beyond the finite, and the infinite here is potential rather than actual. Proclus criticizes those who refer the whole second hypothesis to intellect, since Plato looks at the one itself, not being. He matches the one, whole and end to the back, depth and arch of the Phædrus heaven. The perfective triad follows: having extremes, being perfect with beginning, middle and end, and having figure, straight, round or mixed, under three perfective leaders.","ideas":["One-many, whole-parts and finite-infinite are the three Synoches","Each triad's end is the next triad's beginning","The second hypothesis concerns the one, not intellect","Extremes, the perfect and figure mark the three Teletarchs"],"terms":[{"t":"whole and parts","d":"The middle connective monad, holding many parts in one wholeness."}]},{"i":740,"j":746,"loc":"Book IV, Chapters XXXVIII–XXXIX","title":"How the middle order binds itself and the worlds","summary":"Proclus shows each triad joining its summit to the end of the one above, so the middle order is bound to itself and turns the intellectual to the intelligible Gods. Intellect turns to itself because it is bounded, is perfect, and is a sphere because it first shares in figure. He uses this division to explain other theologians' accounts: the connective Gods are empyrean, ethereal and material, and the Teletarchs form the inerratic, planetary and sublunary realms through circular, spiral and straight figure. In each triad the middle term, difference, whole, the perfect, is characteristic, and each triad reaches toward the infinite at its end, closing the treatment of these Gods.","ideas":["The middle order connects intellectuals to intelligibles","Conversion, perfection and figure come to intellectuals from the perfective triad","The three triads govern the division of the worlds","The triads are monadic, dyadic and triadic"],"terms":[{"t":"empyrean, ethereal, material","d":"The three worlds ruled by the three connective Gods."}]},{"i":747,"j":757,"loc":"Book V, Chapters I–II","title":"The intellectual Gods and their hebdomad","summary":"Book V turns to the intellectual Gods, who end the divine progressions and turn them back to their source. Their life is participated and life-giving, their intelligible coordinate with intellect, and their fatherhood produces divided emanations and divine marriages. Proclus divides them into three fathers, arranged by the intelligible, life and intellect; three undefiled Gods, each joined to one father; and one separating monad, seven in all. This heptad is an intellectual sphere, from which seven hebdomads extend to the lowest orders. Intelligible Gods produce monadically, middle Gods triadically, and intellectual Gods hebdomadically; the Pythagoreans call the heptad light according to intellect.","ideas":["Intellectual life gives life; the earlier life gave being","Three fathers, three undefiled Gods and a separating monad make a heptad","The separating cause is monadic; the others become triadic","Each monad leads a further intellectual hebdomad"],"terms":[{"t":"intellectual Gods","d":"The order following the middle Gods, which generates divine intellect and turns progression back."},{"t":"hebdomad","d":"A group of seven, the characteristic number of the intellectual order."}]},{"i":758,"j":765,"loc":"Book V, Chapters III–IV","title":"Saturn, Rhea, Jupiter, Curetes, and divine separation","summary":"Proclus finds the heptad in Plato. Saturn, called pure intellect in the Cratylus, separates from Heaven and leads the intellectual Gods; Jupiter, with royal soul and intellect, is demiurgic intellect bound to Saturn; Rhea is the life-giving middle. The Curetes of the Laws, guards of Jupiter in Orpheus, are the undefiled triad. The castrations of Heaven and Saturn point to the separating deity; Plato forbids such fables for the many but allows their hidden sense for the wise, since bonds and castrations symbolize communion and separation. In the Timæus, the demiurgus' division of the world-soul into one circle and seven confirms the monad-and-hebdomad structure.","ideas":["Saturn is intelligible; Jupiter is intellectual intellect","The Curetic triad stands for pure, incorruptible life","Plato rejects the surface sense of myths but not their hidden truth","The soul's circles of same and different reflect the intellectual heptad"],"terms":[{"t":"Curetes","d":"Three armed guards of Jupiter, the undefiled intellectual Gods."},{"t":"exection (castration)","d":"In myth, a symbol of the separation of divine orders."}]},{"i":766,"j":778,"loc":"Book V, Chapters V–VII","title":"Saturn as intelligible intellect and the Saturnian age","summary":"Saturn, first king of the intellectual Gods, holds the intelligible of the demiurgic intellect; the Saturnian bonds express this. Since every intellect turning to itself is both intelligible and intellectual, Saturn is more intelligible and Jupiter more intellectual; Proclus accepts Amelius' three intellects, one that is, has and sees. Saturn alone receives and gives rule by force, bounded by Heaven and by Jupiter. Proclus then reads the Politicus myth: the cosmos has a Saturnian and a Jovian life, intellectual and providential. Souls under Saturn are earth-born, grow younger, live naked on spontaneous fruits, symbols of pure intellectual nourishment.","ideas":["What an intellect knows is within it, not outside","Amelius distinguishes intellect that is, has and sees the intelligible","The world's twofold period is Saturnian and Jovian","The golden-age images are read as purification from generation"],"terms":[{"t":"Saturnian period","d":"In the Politicus myth, the age of spontaneous goods, read as the soul's intellectual life."}]},{"i":779,"j":785,"loc":"Book V, Chapters VIII–IX","title":"Saturn's nurslings, his orders, and divine law","summary":"Saturn, the intelligible within the intellectuals, nourishes souls distributively, matching the meadow of the Phædrus. Souls rise from the Saturnian place of survey to the arch, the heaven and the supercelestial place, as theurgy sets lesser mysteries before greater. Saturn constitutes the three kings of the Gorgias, the dæmon rulers of the Laws, the dæmon Gods of the Politicus, and the twofold circulation of the cosmic Gods. Law is with Saturn because law is intellect's distribution; it parallels Adrastia but divides where she guards. The demiurgus looks to it, making fate an image of its division, while souls in either period live under law.","ideas":["Souls are called the nurslings of Saturn","Lower orders always lead souls up to higher ones","Saturn extends his rule from the first Gods down to partial souls","Law divides and distributes; Adrastia guards undivided"],"terms":[{"t":"nomos (law)","d":"The divine distribution of intellect, dwelling with Saturn."}]},{"i":786,"j":795,"loc":"Book V, Chapters X–XI","title":"Saturn's agelessness and the vivific queen Rhea","summary":"Proclus links the Orphic claim that Saturn's beard never turns grey with the Elean guest's myth in the Politicus, where souls under Saturn grow young again. He reads this as the assimilation of souls to a god who is pure intellect and source of the unallured, inflexible triad, and notes that whole souls live in both the Saturnian and Jovian periods at once. He then turns to Rhea, the second intellectual deity and mother of the demiurgus, the middle of the two fathers who receives Saturn's generative power and pours it out as rivers of life. Citing the Cratylus and the Laws, he argues that Plato calls her fontal, and that Ceres is this same middle goddess considered as producing Jupiter and, with him, Proserpine.","ideas":["The Saturnian age's return to youth is read as souls taking on intellectual vigour","Whole souls are at once older and younger, as the one in the Parmenides is","Rhea mediates between Saturn, who gathers multitude, and Jupiter, who divides it","Rhea and Ceres name the same vivific goddess in different relations"],"terms":[{"t":"vivific (life-giving)","d":"Proclus's word for the order of Rhea, which pours life from the intellectual fathers down to the last things."},{"t":"fontal","d":"Belonging to a divine 'fountain', a primary cause from which a whole stream of beings flows."}]},{"i":796,"j":803,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XII–XIII","title":"The demiurgus as third intellectual father","summary":"Proclus begins his account of the demiurgus, the third of the intellectual fathers after Saturn and Rhea. Since it is the mark of intellect to divide and unfold multitude, the source of all division and of the demiurgic gods must be intellectual intellect, analogous to the paradigm at the end of the intelligible order. He then argues by elimination: the maker of wholes cannot be among the supermundane, intelligible, or intelligible-and-intellectual gods, nor can he be Saturn or Rhea. Saturn gathers his offspring back into himself and the vivific goddess is maternal rather than paternal, so the demiurgus must be the third intellectual king.","ideas":["Fabrication is fourfold, and the total fabrication of wholes is the most ancient","The demiurgic monad is to intellectuals what the paradigm is to intelligibles","The paternal and demiurgic belong to bound; the vivific and generative to infinity"],"terms":[{"t":"dēmiourgos (demiurgus)","d":"The maker of the cosmos in Plato's Timaeus, here placed as the last of the intellectual fathers."},{"t":"intellectual intellect","d":"Intellect in its own proper order, the source of division and of distinct forms."}]},{"i":804,"j":812,"loc":"Book V, Chapter XIV","title":"Against splitting the demiurgus into three","summary":"Proclus attacks interpreters who split the Timaeus among three demiurgi of wholes. He grants that there is a demiurgic triad, but holds that every divine order must start from a single monad. Because the world is only-begotten and copies one paradigm, its maker must also be one, whether the paradigm is placed before, in, or after him. The Socratic principle that the many must be comprehended in the one applies more to the gods than to forms. He goes on to show that the demiurgus is neither intelligible nor intelligible-and-intellectual, since Timaeus calls him intellect, has him see and reason, and has him divide by five and seven rather than by threes.","ideas":["Every divine order originates from a pre-existent monad","One paradigm and one world require one demiurgus","The demiurgus's divisions into five and seven mark him as intellectual"],"terms":[{"t":"monad","d":"The single first member of a divine order, from which its multitude proceeds."},{"t":"only-begotten (monogenēs)","d":"Timaeus's description of the world as one and unique, because its paradigm is one."}]},{"i":813,"j":827,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XV–XVI","title":"Intellect itself, fabricator and father","summary":"Proclus argues that because Timaeus calls the demiurgus simply intellect, he is intellectual intellect at the end of the intellectual order, just as the simply intelligible is first among intelligibles; this is why he first makes participated intellect. He then gathers proof from Timaeus' phrase fabricator and father, noting that the fabricative is named before the paternal. A father produces gods and wholes by his very being, a fabricator produces essences and parts by acting. The paternal is primary in the intelligible gods, paternal-and-fabricative in the paradigm, fabricative-and-paternal in the demiurgus, and merely fabricative in the junior gods. The same order of names recurs in the speech to the junior gods and in the Politicus, and father, power and intellect exist in the demiurgus intellectually.","ideas":["Being, life and intellect are in each order, but each order is defined by one of them","Father generates gods and wholes; fabricator gives subsistence to essences and parts","Plato always says 'demiurgus and father', never the reverse","The demiurgus's father, power and intellect mirror bound, infinity and the mixed"],"terms":[{"t":"hyparxis","d":"The proper, defining mode of existence of a divine being."},{"t":"junior gods","d":"The gods of the Timaeus to whom the demiurgus entrusts the making of mortal natures."}]},{"i":828,"j":841,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XVII–XVIII","title":"Demiurgic goodness, reasoning, and words to gods","summary":"Following Timaeus, Proclus explains the demiurgus's goodness as his divinity and his will as what goodness produces; he makes things like himself, holds the paradigmatic cause, and with unconquerable power brings order to matter, which itself comes from gods above him. His reasoning is not search or doubt but intellect's turning to itself, and his discovery is being filled with intelligibles that are naturally visible to him. Proclus then interprets the speech to the junior gods as the outward-going energy of the demiurgic intellect. The speech makes them gods of gods, indissoluble, and immortal in a renewed way, and gives them power to perfect the universe and to generate and dissolve mortal things. Mortal things come from the father but are produced through the junior gods.","ideas":["Divine powers are twofold, abiding within and proceeding outward","Demiurgic 'reasoning' is eternal intellection, not inquiry","The father's words make the junior gods second demiurgi","Mortals come 'from' the demiurgus but 'through' the junior gods"],"terms":[{"t":"renovated immortality","d":"The kind of immortality, named from the Politicus myth, that the junior gods keep through the father's will."}]},{"i":842,"j":850,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XIX–XX","title":"Laws for partial souls; the demiurgus is Jupiter","summary":"Proclus describes the second measures of demiurgic providence: the demiurgus sows partial souls, shows them the laws of Fate, and sets the rewards of virtue and vice. Divine souls hear his words directly, while partial souls receive them at a further remove. He rejects interpreters who give partial and whole souls the same essence, citing the second and third mixtures of the Timaeus. He then lists everything Timaeus credits to the demiurgus, from the world's body and soul to time, the sun, the gods and daemons, and his withdrawal to his accustomed abode. Since Saturn holds the summit of the intellectual triad and Rhea its middle, he concludes that the demiurgus at its end is the great Jupiter.","ideas":["Divine and partial souls receive the demiurgic words in different measures","Partial souls do not share the essence of whole souls","Timaeus' account works as a hymn to the demiurgus","The demiurgus is identified with Jupiter, king at the end of the triad"],"terms":[{"t":"laws of Fate","d":"The laws the demiurgus shows to souls in the Timaeus, governing their descents and rewards."}]},{"i":851,"j":858,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XXI–XXII","title":"Critias and Cratylus: Jupiter as demiurgus","summary":"Proclus begins confirming that Jupiter is the demiurgus from other dialogues. In the Critias, Jupiter, god of gods, gathers the gods to his central dwelling to punish the Atlantics, much as the demiurgus turns the mundane gods to himself. In the Cratylus, Socrates explains Zeus by the two names Dia and Zena, as the one through whom life comes to all things. This matches the Timaeus, where the demiurgus makes the whole world a living animal with a threefold life. The pair of names fits the demiurgic duad: Dia, 'on account of', comes from the paternal summit, and Zena, from life, comes from Rhea's middle order.","ideas":["The Critias assembly of gods mirrors the demiurgus's speech in the Timaeus","Zeus is the cause of life, as the demiurgus is","Both dialogues say the god is hard to know","Two names reflect descent from Saturn (bound) and Rhea (infinity)"],"terms":[{"t":"Dia / Zena","d":"Two Greek forms of Zeus's name, read in the Cratylus as 'on account of whom' and 'life'."}]},{"i":859,"j":867,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XXIII–XXIV","title":"Philebus and Protagoras: royal intellect and politics","summary":"In the Philebus Socrates argues that intellect, working through soul, orders the world, and that Jupiter has a royal soul and royal intellect through the power of cause. Proclus weighs whether Jupiter there is the world itself, and rejects that view held by some interpreters: Jupiter is the cause holding soul and intellect exemptly, receiving intellect from Saturn and soul from Rhea. He then reads the Protagoras myth, in which Prometheus gives the arts from Vulcan and Minerva but cannot enter Jupiter's guarded citadel. Jupiter instead sends Hermes with shame, justice and political science to all. Since the demiurgus founds the first polity in the universe, Proclus argues, the holder of political science is again the demiurgus.","ideas":["Jupiter is the cause of the world's soul and intellect, not the world","The arts derive from Vulcan and Minerva, political science from Jupiter","Jupiter's terrible guards symbolize his unchanging, exempt order","The heavenly polity is the first polity, founded by the demiurgus"],"terms":[{"t":"political science","d":"In the Protagoras myth, the knowledge of justice that Jupiter gives through Hermes to all souls."}]},{"i":868,"j":876,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XXV–XXVI","title":"Politicus and Laws: the two cosmic periods","summary":"From the Politicus myth Proclus takes the twofold circulation of the cosmos: one intellectual and unseen, the other apparent and ruled by Fate. He argues that both Saturn and Jupiter preside over the Saturnian period, Saturn as source of intellectual life and Jupiter as raising souls to Saturn's watch-tower. Jupiter is also the exempt cause of the visible period. Since the Elean guest calls this god demiurgus and father, and the world turns remembering his teaching, Jupiter is again the demiurgus. In the Laws the Athenian guest calls true equality according to proportion the judgment of Jupiter. That analogy is the bond the demiurgus uses to build soul and body in the Timaeus, so Jupiter's nature is demiurgic.","ideas":["The Saturnian period and the Jovian period of Fate and connate desire","Souls nourished by Saturn ascend through Jupiter, as in the Phaedrus","The Politicus keeps the order 'demiurgus and father'","Proportional equality is the judgment of Jupiter and the demiurgus's bond"],"terms":[{"t":"best analogy","d":"Geometric proportion, which the Timaeus calls the finest bond of the world's parts."}]},{"i":877,"j":891,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XXVII–XXIX","title":"Jupiter, the paradigm, and the hidden maker","summary":"Proclus concludes that Jupiter is the demiurgus, and that the paradigm, animal itself, is both united to him and distinct: it contains him intelligibly, and he contains it intellectually. The three intellectual kings correspond to the three intelligible hypostases, and Jupiter matches all-perfect animal. He then asks why Timaeus calls the maker hard to find and impossible to declare to all, while animal itself, ranked above him, is named. His answer is that each order's monad resembles the good, and the demiurgic monad shares something of its unknowability, though only in part. Timaeus treats the paradigm by its forms but the demiurgus by his goodness, and 'best of causes' relates to 'most beautiful' as the good to the beautiful, without making the paradigm lower.","ideas":["Jupiter is intelligibly in animal itself, which is intellectually in Jupiter","Each order's monad is to its series what the good is to all","The demiurgus is hard to know but not wholly ineffable","Goodness, not intellect or life, makes the demiurgus and the sun causes"],"terms":[{"t":"autozōon (animal itself)","d":"The intelligible paradigm of the Timaeus that contains the forms of all living things."}]},{"i":892,"j":903,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XXX–XXXI","title":"The Crater and the genera of being","summary":"Proclus turns to the Crater in which the demiurgus mixes the genera of being to make soul. Essence, motion and rest, and sameness and difference exist first in the three intelligible triads, then in the intelligible-and-intellectual and intellectual orders; Iamblichus and Plotinus are cited along the way. The demiurgus holds the fount of forms and genera and produces three kinds of beings: indivisible intellect, divisible bodies, and soul between them. He makes intellect by himself, soul with the Crater, and body with total Nature and Necessity, which is not matter. The Crater is the fountain of souls. Since the Phaedrus calls soul both fountain and principle of motion, Proclus infers a ruling monad of souls after the fountain and closer to them.","ideas":["The five genera appear in each order in a different mode","Intellect, soul and body come from different co-causes with the demiurgus","The Crater is the fontal cause of all soul","A ruling principle stands nearer to souls than their fountain"],"terms":[{"t":"kratēr (Crater)","d":"The mixing bowl of the Timaeus in which the soul's ingredients are blended."},{"t":"genera of being","d":"Essence, sameness, difference, motion and rest, the ingredients of the soul."}]},{"i":904,"j":911,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XXXII–XXXIII","title":"Rhea's three fountains; the unpolluted triad","summary":"Proclus explains that Rhea's secondary monads, joined to the demiurgus, are three: the fountain of souls, which is the Crater, the fountain of the virtues, and the fountain of Nature. He shows each in the Timaeus: the Crater acts as mother of souls, virtue makes the world a friend to itself, and Nature and the laws of Fate are shown to souls. He finds the language of fountains in the Laws and the Cratylus. He then introduces the unpolluted or inflexible triad, whose three members are joined to Saturn, Rhea and Jupiter. They keep the fathers' powers pure and unchanging, and he finds an image of them in the guardians of the Laws.","ideas":["Soul, virtue and Nature have distinct fontal causes within Rhea","The fountain of Nature is called the first Fate","Each intellectual king has a guardian joined to him"],"terms":[{"t":"unpolluted (achrantos) gods","d":"The guardian triad that keeps the intellectual fathers unmixed with lower things."}]},{"i":912,"j":924,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XXXIV–XXXVI","title":"Guards of Jupiter, the Curetes, and divisions","summary":"Proclus finds the guardian order in the guards around Jupiter's citadel in the Protagoras and in the Cratylus etymology of Saturn as pure intellect, koros. The Saturnian bonds show that the connective gods guard the intellectual kings. The guardianship is triadic, one guardian per king. He identifies the triad with the Curetes of Greek mystery tradition, whose armed dances in Crete the Athenian guest praises in the Laws, together with Minerva, their monad, armed in full panoply. Rhythm and dance signify purity from formless matter. Finally he treats the seventh intellectual monad, the cause of division. He finds it in the mythical sections among the gods, in the Gorgias' separation of Jupiter's reign from Saturn's, and in the demiurgus's cuts of soul in the Timaeus, completing the intellectual hebdomad.","ideas":["Jupiter's terrible guards keep Prometheus and partial gods at a distance","Kōros signifies purity; Minerva is called Korē as cause of purity","Rhythmic dance, even of the heavens, reflects the guardian gods","A dividing monad completes the intellectual hebdomad"],"terms":[{"t":"Curetes","d":"Armed guardian deities of Cretan myth, identified here with the unpolluted triad."},{"t":"hebdomad","d":"The sevenfold intellectual order: three fathers, three guardians, and a dividing monad."}]},{"i":925,"j":938,"loc":"Book V, Chapters XXXVII–XXXVIII","title":"Parmenides on Saturn and Rhea's orders","summary":"Proclus maps the intellectual hebdomad onto the second hypothesis of the Parmenides. The one being 'in itself and in another' marks Saturn: in another as established in the intelligibles before him, which is the higher conversion, and in itself as joined to the first unpolluted monad. Saturn is thus both intellectual father and paternal intellect, a whole who swallows and holds back his offspring. The one being 'moved and at rest' marks the second order. Motion is Rhea's vivific life, which is moved because it receives life from above, and rest is the guardian monad that keeps her steady. As 'in another' precedes 'in itself', so motion precedes rest.","ideas":["Being 'in another' signifies conversion to prior causes and is higher","Being, life and intellect are in each order, each defined by one","Rhea's order is 'that which is moved', filled from higher life","Motion and rest here are divine traits, not genera of being"],"terms":[{"t":"second hypothesis","d":"The Parmenides argument from 'if the one is', read by Proclus as an ordered list of divine ranks."}]},{"i":939,"j":946,"loc":"Book V, Chapter XXXIX","title":"Parmenides on the demiurgic monad","summary":"Proclus turns to the third intellectual order, where the Parmenides relates the one to 'other things', a sign of the body the demiurgus makes, and the conclusions double. 'Same with itself' marks the demiurgus's paternal union and bound. 'Same with others' marks his generative power present in all he makes. 'Different from others' marks the guardian that keeps him pure and exempt, and 'different from itself' marks the seventh, dividing monad that separates him from his father. Proclus then lists the sign of each of the seven intellectual monads. Plato puts union before division and purity before providence because the gods are present to all things while exempt from them.","ideas":["'Other things' signals the demiurgus's relation to bodies","The doubled conclusions match the demiurgus's double name","Each of the seven intellectual monads has a Parmenidean sign"],"terms":[]},{"i":947,"j":960,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters I–II","title":"The ruling supermundane gods introduced","summary":"Book VI begins with the ruling or leading gods, who come next after the demiurgus and are turned toward him. Proclus defends the terms fountain and principle from the Phaedrus: fountains are self-begotten and first-effective, while principles are unbegotten and stand above everything generated. He then reviews the whole procession: the intelligible gods in triads, the intelligible-and-intellectual gods, and the intellectual gods in hebdomads. After these come the ruling gods, who take their subsistence from the one fabrication and preside over participated intellect. Below them are the liberated gods, between the supermundane and the mundane, and then the mundane gods. The series is continuous because the first cause is one and good, so the end of each order joins the start of the next.","ideas":["Fountain and principle differ: self-begotten vs. unbegotten","Liberated gods mediate between supermundane and mundane gods","The ends of higher orders join the beginnings of lower ones"],"terms":[{"t":"hēgemonikoi (ruling/leading gods)","d":"The supermundane order that comes next after the intellectual gods and is exempt from the world."},{"t":"liberated gods","d":"Gods between the supermundane and mundane, touching the world yet not part of it."}]},{"i":961,"j":970,"loc":"Book VI, Chapter III","title":"The ruling gods as cause of likeness","summary":"Proclus names the common peculiarity of the ruling gods: they assimilate, making lower things like higher ones and equals like each other. All images, all procession and all conversion depend on likeness. The demiurgus holds the causes of likeness united, since his being lies in sameness, but the ruling gods unfold them into division. Likeness keeps forms stable through the cycle of generation and completes the world as an image of intelligible animal. Proclus holds that likeness and unlikeness are not single intellectual forms. They correspond to sameness and difference and exist first in the gods, which is why the Parmenides derives like and unlike from the one. The last intelligible triad is thus the source of the fontal and ruling gods.","ideas":["Likeness is the defining mark of the ruling gods","Every procession and conversion happens through similitude","Like and unlike exist first in the gods, then in forms"],"terms":[{"t":"homoiōsis (assimilation)","d":"Making a thing like its cause or paradigm, the proper work of the ruling gods."}]},{"i":971,"j":981,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters IV–V","title":"The assimilative powers and the ruling Gods' divisions","summary":"Proclus lists the assimilative powers in order: those that unfold the father's intellectual production, those that hold wholes together, those that lead secondary natures to perfection, and those that collect divided things into monads, among others. Through this order matter, generation, the heavens and time itself are made like their higher causes, and similitude binds causes and effects into one sympathetic chain reaching down to stones and plants. In Chapter V he divides the ruling Gods into a top, middle and last rank. He traces the middle rank to the three intellectual fathers, giving paternal, generative and elevating triads, with an unpolluted guardian order joined to each.","ideas":["Every procession and every conversion happens through similitude","Similitude makes the world a single sympathetic whole in which contraries are bound together","Each God's chain of followers bears its monad's stamp, clearer near the top and fainter below","The ruling Gods divide into paternal, prolific, elevating and guardian orders"],"terms":[{"t":"assimilative Gods (ruling leaders)","d":"The order just below the intellectual Gods that makes all secondary things like their principles."},{"t":"hyparxis","d":"The summit or highest mode of existence of a divine nature."}]},{"i":982,"j":990,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters VI–VII","title":"The three demiurgi and the one demiurgus","summary":"Proclus argues that after the demiurgic monad comes a triad of ruling fathers, Jupiter, Neptune and Pluto, whom the Gorgias calls sons of Saturn dividing their father's kingdom. He criticises interpreters who make Saturn the demiurgus, or who make the first of the three the maker of the whole. Each of the three acts on the whole world, but in its own way: the first gives essence, the second motion, life and generation, the third division and conversion. The Timæus demiurgus does all of this at once and without division, and he also governs souls' descent and return, so he is exempt from the triad and not counted among them.","ideas":["A monad must stand above the multitude that proceeds from it","Saturn, who keeps his offspring within himself, cannot be the demiurgus","First, second and third fathers cause being, life and conversion","The three also govern souls before, during and after generation"],"terms":[{"t":"demiurgus","d":"The maker and father of the universe in the Timæus, here identified with the intellectual Jupiter."},{"t":"monad and triad","d":"A unifying source and the threefold multitude it produces and holds within itself."}]},{"i":991,"j":999,"loc":"Book VI, Chapter VIII","title":"The twofold Jupiter and Justice his attendant","summary":"From the Gorgias Proclus concludes that there are two Jupiters: the intellectual demiurgus who stands before the three fathers, and the first of Saturn's three sons, at the head of the ruling triad. He says the three proceed from Saturn as the source they come 'from' and from Jupiter as the one 'by' whom they are brought forth. The Philebus, the Politicus and the Cratylus are cited for the higher Jupiter's royal intellect and his bond with his father. The Athenian guest's God, who holds beginning, middle and end and is followed by Justice, is taken to be this whole demiurgus, and Orphic verses are quoted in support.","ideas":["The great Jupiter of the triad shares the demiurgus's name through close union with him","The three are said to divide Saturn's kingdom, but they proceed through Jupiter","Bounding 'in a direct path' and moving circularly first belong to the demiurgus","Holding beginning, middle and end implies the power to generate the triad"],"terms":[{"t":"Dikē (Justice)","d":"The goddess who follows the demiurgus and punishes those who break the divine law."},{"t":"aph' hou / huph' hou","d":"The 'from which' (hidden source) and 'by which' (visible producer) of a procession."}]},{"i":1000,"j":1009,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters IX–X","title":"Second laws and the realms of the three fathers","summary":"Proclus reads the straight path as the procession of wholes from the demiurgus and the circle as their return to him. Paternal cause, power and intellect, the Timæus triad, match beginning, middle and end. In the Gorgias the divine law is shared by Saturn and the great Jupiter, while the three sons need secondary laws and judges suited to divided lives. Chapter X then sets out the fathers' allotments several ways: Jupiter, Neptune and Pluto rule the fixed sphere, the planets and the sublunary region respectively; then fire and air, water, and earth; then heights, hollows and underworld; then the eastern, middle and western centres.","ideas":["Straight motion stands for procession and circular motion for conversion","Saturnian and Jovian law is one; the sons of Saturn rule by more varied laws","Souls not yet born are Jovian, souls in generation fall under Neptune, souls being purified under Pluto","Pluto is called terrestrial Jupiter"],"terms":[{"t":"allotment","d":"The region or share of the universe a God governs."}]},{"i":1010,"j":1019,"loc":"Book VI, Chapter XI","title":"The vivific triad of Core","summary":"From Rhea, the vivific fountain, comes a life-giving triad among the ruling Gods, which Plato calls by the one name Core and joins with Ceres. Core is twofold: above the world she stays with her mother and is joined with Jupiter, and within the world she is carried off by Pluto and gives life to the lowest things. Neptune alone of the brothers has no union with her, because he already holds the vivific middle. Her three monads are Diana, Proserpine and Minerva (Hecate, Soul and Virtue among the Chaldeans), which Proclus finds in the Cratylus etymology of Pherephatta. Because soul and virtue first exist in these Gods, souls are immortal through their likeness to the divine.","ideas":["The ruling Gods' triads mirror Saturn, Rhea and Jupiter","Proserpine above with Ceres; Proserpine below as Pluto's wife","Pherephatta's name signifies wisdom, contact with what is generated, and awe-inspiring power","Virtue is likeness to the Gods, and such likeness comes from the assimilative rulers"],"terms":[{"t":"Core (Kore)","d":"The Virgin, Proserpine, the source of the partial forms of life."},{"t":"vivific","d":"Life-giving; the property of the middle, Rhea-derived order of Gods."}]},{"i":1020,"j":1032,"loc":"Book VI, Chapter XII","title":"The Apolloniacal or solar elevating triad","summary":"The third ruling triad turns all things back to their principle and comes from the solar fountain inside the demiurgus; Plato names it all at once as Apolloniacal. Proclus argues from the Laws (a temple shared by the sun and Apollo), the Cratylus and the Republic's analogy of sun and good that Plato, like Orpheus, treats Apollo and the sun as one. From the four meanings in Apollo's name (simplicity and truth, loosing and purifying, shooting arrows, revolving) he derives the solar monads: one reveals truth, one destroys disorder, one harmonises, and a fourth pure cause protects them. The Timæus account of the sun's specially kindled light shows that it ranks above the world, and the dexter and sinister hands make up a demiurgic duad.","ideas":["Three lights: the good's for intelligibles, Apollo's for intellectuals, the sun's for sensibles","Apollo's single name gathers many powers into one","The demiurgus himself kindles the sun's light, which marks a transcendence above the world","The sun, offspring of the good, gives the world's natures their measure of happiness"],"terms":[{"t":"Pæonian powers","d":"Apollo's healing and purifying powers, matched to the last solar monad."},{"t":"demiurgic duad","d":"The two 'hands' of the solar order, right and left, that govern the twofold arrangements of the world."}]},{"i":1033,"j":1041,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters XIII–XIV","title":"Corybantes, and the similar and dissimilar","summary":"Proclus says that among the ruling Gods there is an unpolluted guardian order, the Corybantes who guard Core all round, corresponding to the Curetes among the intellectual Gods. He finds hints of it in Plato's references to Corybantic piping and enthronement and to the Curetes' armed dances, and connects the name with 'koron', purity. He then turns to the Parmenides. Sameness and difference mark the demiurgic order, and from them come similitude and dissimilitude, the marks of the assimilative order: similitude matches bound, the paternal and converting causes, while dissimilitude matches the infinite and the prolific causes. Each has one origin that is known only to intellect and one that is plain.","ideas":["The guardian triad keeps the prolific powers unchanged as they proceed","Similitude unites and dissimilitude separates processions","The similar comes from sameness and difference together, and so does the dissimilar","In the Gods the ineffable comes before the effable"],"terms":[{"t":"Corybantes","d":"The unpolluted guardian Gods of the ruling order, protectors of Core."},{"t":"to homoion / to anomoion","d":"The similar and the dissimilar, the Parmenides' marks of the assimilative Gods."}]},{"i":1042,"j":1050,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters XV–XVI","title":"The liberated Gods between supermundane and mundane","summary":"Proclus introduces the liberated Gods, who sit between the ruling (supermundane) Gods and the mundane Gods. They are both separate from the world and arranged together with it, and they bind the two ranks together, as middle orders do at every level. He explains their names: liberated, supercelestial, undefiled, elevating, perfect. As the intelligible king makes triads, the demiurgus produces three partial orders: ruling, liberated and mundane. Citing the Elean guest's two cosmic periods and the Phædrus soul that governs the whole world, Proclus argues that the power to act across the whole world, free of a single allotment, reaches mundane Gods and souls only from the liberated Gods.","ideas":["Every divine level has a middle that binds its extremes","Ruling, liberated and mundane Gods are the three partial orders","Gods have two kinds of activity: caring for what they govern, and rising with the liberated Gods","An unencumbered, world-wide activity is the gift of the liberated Gods"],"terms":[{"t":"liberated Gods (apolytoi)","d":"Gods both above the world and joined with it, freed from division into parts."},{"t":"mundane Gods","d":"Gods who complete the sensible world and hold perpetual allotments in it."}]},{"i":1051,"j":1062,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters XVII–XVIII","title":"The liberated Gods' essence and the Phædrus dodecad","summary":"Proclus argues that the soul-like property is hidden in the supermundane Gods, more visible in the liberated, and fully shown in the mundane. He ties three ranks of souls to essence, sameness and difference. He lists the liberated Gods' kinds (transporters, elevators, colligators, undefiled, perfective, prolific) and the activities that lift mundane things toward intellect. In the Phædrus, he says, Plato measures this order by the dodecad: the twelve leaders, headed by the Jovian and Vesta monads with a decad that includes Apollo, Venus and Mars, lead Gods, dæmons and souls to the vision of intelligibles. Twelve is a symbol of all-perfect procession, not a count of units.","ideas":["The soul-like property grows more manifest at each lower rank","Souls with the ruling, liberated and mundane Gods are marked by essence, sameness and difference","Prophetic, amatory and divisive lives come from Apollo, Venus and Mars","The liberated dodecad links the supermundane and celestial dodecads"],"terms":[{"t":"dodecad","d":"The number twelve, taken as the measure of the liberated Gods' complete procession."},{"t":"psychical peculiarity","d":"The soul-property that divine orders acquire as they descend toward the world."}]},{"i":1063,"j":1071,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters XIX–XX","title":"Locating the Phædrus Jupiter among liberated Gods","summary":"Proclus asks where the Phædrus's great leader, who drives a winged chariot, belongs. He cannot be the demiurgus, who has no counterpart such as Vesta and contains all lives without division. He cannot be mundane, since he is called great, as Love is a great dæmon. He cannot be the assimilative Jupiter, who leads a division into three, not an army in eleven parts. So he is liberated: in the heavens yet exempt, and the whole twelve belong to this order. Chapter XX traces the dodecad to two sources: three from the demiurgic division into first, middle and last, times four from the assimilative Gods' paternal, prolific, elevating and guardian kinds.","ideas":["Effects cannot be divided off as equals to their cause","'Great' signals a transcendence above the mundane rank","The army in eleven parts rules out the ruling triad","Twelve arises as three times four"],"terms":[{"t":"winged chariot","d":"Jupiter's vehicle in the Phædrus, read as an intellectual, undivided soul."}]},{"i":1072,"j":1079,"loc":"Book VI, Chapters XXI–XXII","title":"Jupiter, Vesta and the four triads of twelve","summary":"Within the twelve, Jupiter and Vesta rule. Jupiter is not the world's intellect but a liberated God, who gives motion and the ascent to the intelligible. Vesta, who is not the earth, gives stable and undefiled permanence: the fixed poles and axes, the earth resting at the centre. Their roles divide Saturn's double conversion, to himself and to his principles. The liberated Gods are both rulers and arranged. Chapter XXII names the four triads: demiurgic (Jupiter, Neptune, Vulcan), guardian (Vesta, Minerva, Mars), vivific (Ceres, Juno, Diana), and elevating (Hermes, Venus, Apollo). Their chariots are intellectual supermundane souls, and in them time is always with eternity.","ideas":["Vesta grants abiding in oneself; Jupiter grants rising to first natures","The liberated Gods both lead and take part in order","Hermes elevates through philosophy, Venus through love, Apollo through music","Jupiter's chariot is undivided; mundane Gods have horses and charioteers"],"terms":[{"t":"Vesta (Hestia)","d":"Leader of the guardian triad, the source of stability in the cosmos."},{"t":"anagogic","d":"Elevating; leading souls upward to their principles."}]},{"i":1080,"j":1089,"loc":"Book VI, Chapter XXIII","title":"Necessity and the Fates in the Republic","summary":"Proclus reads the myth of Er as setting a monad, Necessity, above a triad, the Fates. Necessity moves the heavens by her mere being: she sits in the intellectual order like Adrastia and guards what the demiurgus makes. The Fates touch and leave off touching the spindle, which shows the liberated rank: they are joined with the heavens yet separate, and the myth's 'for a time' veils a difference of properties. Following the Laws, he ranks Lachesis first, Clotho second and Atropos third, matched to past, present and future as degrees of comprehension, and supports this from the hands and the souls' lots and descent. Their thrones, crowns, white robes and songs show their supercelestial order.","ideas":["Necessity governs by intellect, not by force","Touching and not touching coexist in divine natures, not one after another in time","The past contains present and future, so Lachesis contains the other two","Each symbol in the myth, such as thrones, garlands and robes, marks a rank"],"terms":[{"t":"Anankē (Necessity)","d":"Mother of the Fates, on whose knees the spindle of the universe turns."},{"t":"Moirai (Parcæ)","d":"The Fates Lachesis, Clotho and Atropos, here placed among the liberated Gods."}]},{"i":1090,"j":1094,"loc":"Book VI, Chapter XXIV","title":"Touching and not touching in the Parmenides","summary":"Proclus takes the Parmenides conclusion that the one touches and does not touch itself and others as the mark of the liberated Gods. Contact means providence joined with what it governs, and lack of contact means exempt rule; he cites Pherephatta's contact with generation and the Phædo's soul touching being. The one touches itself because it holds a multitude in itself, and does not because it is united. It touches others because it is in them, yet shares no common number with them. He derives these Gods from the One, from the unpolluted Gods (being in itself), and from the demiurgic and assimilative orders (being in others), and ends Book VI.","ideas":["Contact signifies coordinated providence; non-contact signifies separate rule","The liberated one is both uniform and multiplied","Being in itself here ranks above being in others, the reverse of the first procession","The liberated Gods are exempt from sensibles as the one is exempt from intelligibles"],"terms":[{"t":"in itself / in another","d":"Parmenides conclusions that mark conversion to self and relation to what is outside."}]},{"i":1095,"j":1242,"loc":"Endnotes 1–147","title":"Translator's endnotes: emendations and glosses, first part","summary":"The translator's endnotes to the treatise begin. Most propose corrections to the printed Greek, supplying omitted words or replacing one reading with another, sometimes following the Latin version of Portus. Others gloss terms: hyparxis is the summit or flower of an essence, 'uniform' means characterised by unity, and the 'adyta' are the hidden order of Night and Phanes. One long note quotes Hermeas's commentary on the Phædrus on how telete, muesis and epopteia differ as stages of initiation. Several notes mark lines too corrupt to translate.","ideas":["Most notes repair the Greek text, often by single words","Short glosses explain technical vocabulary","Hermeas on telete, muesis and epopteia as stages of initiation","Some corrupt passages are left untranslated"],"terms":[{"t":"epopteia","d":"The highest stage of initiation, being established as a spectator of the mysteries."}]},{"i":1243,"j":1384,"loc":"Endnotes 148–289","title":"Translator's endnotes: emendations and scholia, second part","summary":"The endnotes continue, mostly with corrections to the Greek (for example, exēirētai changed to exērtētai, or noeros to noētos). A few explain points: morphe concerns colour, figure and magnitude, and Plato calls every body that circulates forever a 'divine generated nature'. Two notes reproduce marginal remarks by a Greek scholiast, one on the partial demiurgic triad and the perfective Gods, one on what 'intellectual' and 'essence' mean in Chapter XVII. Another gloss explains 'from which' as a hidden procession and 'by which' as a manifest one.","ideas":["Continued word-level corrections of the Greek","A scholiast ties the straight and circular to the perfective triad","A scholiast glosses Proclus's terms in Chapter XVII","One note's position is unmarked in the original edition"],"terms":[{"t":"morphe","d":"Shape as it relates to the colour, figure and magnitude of surfaces."}]},{"i":1385,"j":1399,"loc":"Seventh Book (added), Chapters I–II","title":"Added book: the mundane Gods and their allotments","summary":"A supplementary Seventh Book, written to replace a lost book of Proclus, takes up the mundane Gods. The world images the intelligible Gods, and twelve Gods arrange it in four triads: makers, animators, harmonisers and guardians. Their statues show this, and the elements and spheres are assigned to them. Chapter II divides the mundane Gods into celestial and sublunary, gives each planet its power, and explains the cosmic divisions by two, three, four, five, seven and twelve. On how sublunary allotments can be perpetual, it answers that space, the ethereal vehicles and elemental wholes do not change. A God appears in a place only when that place becomes fit to receive him, and Iamblichus's puzzle about Minerva's allotment of Athens is answered the same way.","ideas":["Twelve mundane Gods make, animate, harmonise and guard the world","Sun, Venus and Mercury reveal truth, beauty and symmetry","Divine allotments stay fixed; only the fitness of what receives them changes","A God's 'birthday' is a change in the participants, not in the God"],"terms":[{"t":"epitēdeiotēs (aptitude)","d":"A place's or body's fitness to receive a God's illumination."},{"t":"holotēs (wholeness)","d":"The permanent whole of an element, whose parts change."}]},{"i":1400,"j":1413,"loc":"Seventh Book (added), Chapters III–IV","title":"Incorporeal Gods and the visible celestial gods","summary":"The added book argues that the mundane Gods take nothing from bodies. They govern bodies from outside and give without receiving, since otherwise bodies would rank higher than Gods. The comparison is sunlight, which is present in the air without mixing with it and leaves nothing behind when its source goes; in the same way divine light is everywhere whole and indivisible. Chapter IV asks how the visible orbs can be Gods. The answer is that celestial bodies depend on divine souls and do not contain them, and that these bodies are simple, unchanging and move in one circle. Their Gods are rooted in the intelligible world, so all the Gods share one unbroken union.","ideas":["Higher natures contain lower ones, never the reverse","Divine presence is like light, not like a mixture","Celestial bodies imitate divine unity, sameness and life","Visible and intelligible Gods are joined in one union"],"terms":[{"t":"visible statues of the Gods","d":"The celestial orbs, generated from and established in the intelligible Gods."}]},{"i":1414,"j":1421,"loc":"Chapter V","title":"The demiurgus addresses the mundane Gods","summary":"The translator quotes the Timæus speech of the demiurgus to the junior Gods and introduces extracts from Proclus's commentary on it. Proclus says the speech's aim is to give the mundane Gods demiurgic power and providence and lead them to generate the remaining mortal animals, and he praises its grand, concise style. He compares Homer's Jupiter, who gathers the Gods either directly or through Themis, and argues that Plato's one speech holds both modes: the Gods stay unmingled by imitating the father and become providential by being told to fabricate. The chapter ends with Syrianus's explanation of \"Gods of Gods\": each mundane God is an unapparent divinity joined to an apparent, bodily one.","ideas":["The father makes all things at once; the Gods he produces make later natures together with him","Gods remain exempt from what they make yet provide for it","Mundane Gods are two-fold, unapparent and apparent, so they are \"Gods of Gods\""],"terms":[{"t":"demiurgus","d":"The maker and father of the universe who speaks to the junior Gods in the Timæus."},{"t":"Themis","d":"In Homer, the Goddess through whom Jupiter calls the Gods to council; for Proclus she holds the divine laws of providence."}]},{"i":1422,"j":1427,"loc":"Chapter VI","title":"Whom the speech addresses and three demiurgic energies","summary":"Proclus argues that the opening words deify their hearers and that the speech is addressed not to intellects, bodies or souls alone but to the composite of divine soul and animal, which knows the father's will through reason. He then explains \"of whom I am the demiurgus and father\" as a triple energy: deifying, giving indissoluble connection, and likening the Gods to animal itself. These three energies set the junior Gods in the three intelligible triads: the one being, eternity, and all-perfect animal. The father's will is called the strongest bond, and the chapter ends by distinguishing father alone, father and maker, maker and father (Jupiter), and maker alone.","ideas":["Every mundane God has a body-animal, a divine soul and a separate intellect","Things made by an immovable cause alone are indissoluble","The demiurgic will is a greater bond than nature or power","Four grades: father, father-maker (Phanes), maker-father (Jupiter), maker"],"terms":[{"t":"intelligible triads","d":"The three highest orders (one being, eternity, animal itself) in which the junior Gods are established."},{"t":"Phanes Protogonus","d":"Orphic name for the father and maker at the extremity of the intelligible order."}]},{"i":1428,"j":1434,"loc":"Chapter VII","title":"How the mundane Gods are dissoluble and indissoluble","summary":"Proclus comments on \"every thing which is bound is dissoluble.\" He argues that every bond implies a possible separation, since a bond holds many things that keep their own natures, and he distinguishes four grades: indissoluble from itself and another (intelligibles), two middle grades (the mundane Gods), and dissoluble from itself and another (mortals). The mundane Gods are indissoluble as works of the father yet dissoluble because they are composed of simples whose causes he holds. Because the universe is well composed and beautifully harmonized, only its maker could dissolve it, and he will not, since that belongs to an evil nature. Proclus concludes that the mundane Gods are incorruptible in their own nature.","ideas":["A bond is many things united while each keeps its nature","Bodies are bound by analogy, souls by ratios, animals by animated bonds","Well, beautifully, harmonized: final, paradigmatic and demiurgic causes","The universe cannot be dissolved, since its good maker will not undo it"],"terms":[{"t":"bond (desmos)","d":"A power holding many things together while each is preserved; every bond carries a matching mode of dissolution."}]},{"i":1435,"j":1442,"loc":"Chapter VIII","title":"The middle immortality of mundane Gods","summary":"Proclus explains \"so far as you are generated, you are not immortal\": the mundane Gods stand as a mean between extremes. What is primarily immortal gives itself immortality; what receives endless life from another is \"not immortal\" yet not mortal; what has endless life from neither is mortal. The same three-step scale applies to the indissoluble. Citing Love in the Banquet, he sets out a ladder: eternal immortality of intelligibles, the always-becoming immortality of mundane Gods, dæmons who descend and return without oblivion, partial souls who forget in descending, and the merely mortal.","ideas":["Whatever is primarily something is so from itself","Mundane Gods receive immortality from another, so they are a mean","Dæmons are truly middle between Gods and partial souls"],"terms":[{"t":"partial souls","d":"Individual souls such as ours, whose immortality suffers oblivion in descent."},{"t":"dæmons","d":"Attendants of the Gods who descend and return in intellectual energy without forgetting."}]},{"i":1443,"j":1451,"loc":"Chapter IX","title":"Learning the father's will; three mortal genera","summary":"Proclus reads \"Learn now therefore what I say to you\" as filling the mundane Gods with demiurgic conceptions from intelligible animal, after the earlier words deified them and made them indissoluble. On \"three genera of mortals yet remain,\" he explains that the intelligible ideas of winged, aquatic and pedestrian animals are divided in the intellectual order, so the father makes divine animals himself and gives mortal ones to the junior Gods. Animal itself produces silently, the demiurgus by speaking (thinking), and the Gods through motion. Mortal natures must exist so that all that can be may be and the world be complete, which he links with the Orphic story of Proserpine's unfinished webs.","ideas":["Mortal things need movable causes; perpetual ones come from the immovable cause","Three causes of mortals: animal itself, the demiurgus's word, the Gods' motion","No mortal genus is celestial, since the first of each order is undefiled"],"terms":[{"t":"animal itself","d":"The intelligible paradigm containing the causes of all kinds of animals."},{"t":"mathesis (learning)","d":"Instruction suited to soul, by which the Gods receive the father's will."}]},{"i":1452,"j":1462,"loc":"Chapters X–XI","title":"The command to fabricate; why \"junior\" Gods","summary":"Proclus explains the command \"convert yourselves... to the fabrication of animals\" as having two aims: providence toward mortals and the completion of the universe in likeness to all-perfect animal. The words excite the Gods, above all the sun, to make through motion and according to nature, imitating the father's power, and Proclus notes that the demiurgus divides mortals by genera, not by ideas of individuals. Chapter XI turns to a later Timæus passage in which the father hands the junior Gods mortal bodies and the rest of the human soul. Proclus offers several reasons for the name \"junior,\" preferring that their monad Bacchus is the recent God and that they always come into being in time.","ideas":["Superabundant power is prolific of lower things","There are no ideas of Socrates or Plato as particulars","Junior Gods are perpetually generated, not begun at a time","They hold demiurgic, vivific and perfective powers over mortal life"],"terms":[{"t":"second fabrication","d":"The making done by the junior Gods, following and imitating the father's first, exempt fabrication."},{"t":"Bacchus","d":"Named the monad of the second fabrication and the recent God."}]},{"i":1463,"j":1471,"loc":"Chapters XII–XIII","title":"Gods cause no evil; the two fabrications","summary":"Proclus comments on the order to govern the mortal animal so it is not the cause of evil to itself. He argues that no celestial God is malignant; true evil is vice of the soul, which we cause ourselves, and the stars only signify, while recipients pervert good gifts, as Plotinus and Homer's Jupiter say. On \"he remained in his own accustomed manner,\" he reads the father's abiding as eternal establishment in the one. Chapter XIII sets out contrasts between the first fabrication and the second, whose Gods borrow fire, earth, water and air from the world and bind them with dissoluble nails. Parts are returned to their wholes, so generation and corruption never stop.","ideas":["Disease and poverty are not evils; vice is","Celestial gifts are received badly by unfit recipients","The father abides; his children learn, understand and imitate","Borrowed elements must be restored so wholes are not used up"],"terms":[{"t":"unapparent fabrication","d":"The father's making by intellection alone, which the junior Gods unfold into visible works."}]},{"i":1472,"j":1485,"loc":"Chapters XIV–XV","title":"Fixed stars, planets, moon, Mercury, Venus, sun","summary":"The translator turns to the celestial Gods. The inerratic sphere is a monad over the many fixed stars, while each planet leads a hidden multitude of satellites (Gods, dæmons and partial souls), so each sphere is a world. Noting the loss of Proclus's seventh book, he then gathers what Platonic writings say of each planet: the moon as mother of generation, Mercury as giver of symmetry and discipline, Venus as cause of beauty. Through Proclus's commentary on the Timæus he argues the sun measures visible time and has a two-fold generation, as one planet and as a supermundane ruler analogous to the good. All the heavens take their light from the sun.","ideas":["Each planetary sphere contains satellites analogous to the fixed stars","Mercury presides over gymnastic, music and the disciplines","The sun is a manifest measure of time","The sun is mundane as a planet but supermundane as leader of wholes"],"terms":[{"t":"kosmokratores (governors of the world)","d":"The planets, allotted total power and each leading its own multitude."},{"t":"inerratic sphere","d":"The sphere of the fixed stars, which Plato calls a true world."}]},{"i":1486,"j":1494,"loc":"Chapter XVI","title":"Julian's oration on the sovereign sun","summary":"The translator gives an extract from the emperor Julian's oration to the sun as agreeing with Plato. Julian argues that light is the incorporeal form of the diaphanous, whose source is the pure energy of intellect, and that light perfects both sight and the visible. From the sun's making day and night, ruling the planets, and lighting the moon, he infers that it is king among the intellectual Gods and a medium between intelligible purity and sensible bodies. He adds that the sun moves in the starless sphere, divides the zodiac into twelve and thirty-six powers, and frees souls from body, giving them a vehicle of divine splendour.","ideas":["Light is incorporeal and cannot come from a body","The sun's middle position mirrors its middle rank among intellectual Gods","Solar light remains unmixed and undefiled on earth","The sun nourishes us and raises souls"],"terms":[{"t":"diaphanous","d":"The transparent medium underlying the elements, made actual by light."}]},{"i":1495,"j":1516,"loc":"Chapters XVII–XVIII","title":"Apollo's four powers and the Muses","summary":"From Proclus's scholia on the Cratylus, the translator explains Apollo's name as the cause of union. Socrates names four powers, medicinal, prophetic, arrow-darting and harmonic, which Proclus says appear most in the sublunary realm, the heavens, the liberated Gods and the supermundane order respectively. Each power brings multitude to unity, and all are held in Jupiter first, so Apollo is not the demiurgic intellect; his purifying power is likened to the good of the Republic. Chapter XVIII treats the Muses: Apollo is the monad of cosmic harmony, the nine Muses its number, and the name comes from inquiry. It ends with Hermeas's reading of the four Muses in the Phædrus myth of the grasshoppers.","ideas":["Health, truth, order and harmony all reduce many to one","Apollo unites by monad, the Muses distribute by number","Philosophy is the greatest music","Dance in Terpsichore's sense is living in symphony with the universe"],"terms":[{"t":"Musagetes","d":"Apollo as leader of the Muses."},{"t":"telestic art","d":"The art of mystic rites that links last participants to the Gods through sympathy."}]},{"i":1517,"j":1526,"loc":"Chapters XIX–XX","title":"Mars, Jupiter, Saturn; celestial gifts not evil","summary":"The translator describes the triad above the sun: Mars divides and moves contraries, Jupiter gives royal and political life, and Saturn, slow and old, is the source of intellect. Proclus comments on the Timæus line that the planets' bodies became animals through vital bonds and learned their order. Each has a separable, intellectual life by which it is a God and a life divided about body by which it is an animal; its one, intellect and soul are God in three degrees, and it shares in making time. Chapter XX argues that the celestial powers descend unchanged but generation receives them in its own disordered way, so harm comes from the recipients.","ideas":["Mars needs Venus to bring order to contraries","The true sun and true Jupiter differ from their visible orbs","Planetary periods are parts of the whole of time","What is salutary to the whole may harm a part"],"terms":[{"t":"vital bonds","d":"Animated bonds by which the planetary bodies are held in their souls."}]},{"i":1527,"j":1544,"loc":"Chapter XXI","title":"Minerva, philosophic and philopolemic","summary":"Following Proclus on the Timæus, the translator explains why Minerva is called both lover of war and lover of wisdom. She is one of the first intellectual unities in the demiurgus, born from his head, and proceeds through the orders of Proserpine, the liberated Gods, the heavens and the earth. She is philosophic as separate, immaterial wisdom and philopolemic as ruling over all contrariety, as Homer's veil and warlike robe show. Iamblichus reads her shield as impassive guard and her spear as power cutting through matter. Her allotment reaches the sun, moon and earth, which explains how one Goddess has both Athens and Saïs, and the Saïs inscription is quoted.","ideas":["Minerva connects contraries, while Mars separates them","Her titles include Phosphoros, Saviour, Ergane and Virgin","One divine power can be shared by many places differently"],"terms":[{"t":"theonoē","d":"Socrates's etymology of Minerva in the Cratylus: deific intellection."},{"t":"philopolemic","d":"War-loving; for Proclus, ruling over and binding contrariety."}]},{"i":1545,"j":1550,"loc":"Chapter XXII","title":"Earth as divine animal and our nurse","summary":"From Proclus, the translator explains Plato's earth as proceeding from the intelligible and intellectual Earth. The true earth is neither its bulk nor its soul alone but a divine animal: intellect, soul, ethereal body and visible mass. It is our nurse because it holds terrestrial animals as heaven holds divine ones and because it feeds our bodies, souls and intellects. Its being conglobed about the pole is read as the axis, a Titanic power named Atlas, or with Iamblichus as the heavens. Earth makes and guards day and night; some call her Isis or Ceres, and terrestrial forms of many Gods surround her one divinity.","ideas":["Wholes must be animated before their parts","The earth nourishes us according to all of herself","The axis stands for a divine power that holds the universe together","All celestial Gods have terrestrial progressions"],"terms":[{"t":"pole","d":"Read as the universe's axis, or with Iamblichus as the heavens about which earth is conglobed."}]},{"i":1551,"j":1558,"loc":"Chapter XXIII","title":"Why earth is the most ancient God","summary":"Proclus answers those who look only at earth's dark bulk, pointing to her stability, generative power, concord with heaven and central position, the Pythagorean tower of Jupiter. He cites the Phædo's true earth of twelve skins containing rivers of all elements, and the pairing of earth and fire with the moon as \"celestial earth.\" Dignity is judged by powers, not places, and by conversion the centre is joined to the poles. Earth holds Tartarus and the Saturnian power, and mirrors the intellectual Earth's perfective, guardian and Titanic orders. The chapter closes with Theophrastus calling earth the common Vesta of Gods and men.","ideas":["Earth alone among elements contains all elements, like a world","The heavens are fiery, generation earthy","Things are judged by essence and powers, not place","Last things keep the analogy of first ones"],"terms":[{"t":"kourotrophos","d":"\"Nourisher of youth,\" Athenian title of earth as nurse."}]},{"i":1559,"j":1571,"loc":"Chapters XXIV–XXV","title":"Sublunary Gods and the children of Gods","summary":"The translator turns to the sublunary Gods, whom Plato calls dæmons, quoting the Timæus genealogy from Heaven and Earth to Jupiter and Juno and noting the gaps in Proclus's commentary. Proclus explains why speaking of them is beyond our ability: physiologists denied providence below the moon, and souls forget what is near more than what is high. He argues from the telestic art that the demiurgus must have set Gods and dæmons over the elements, down to guardians of individuals. Chapter XXV places them after the celestial Gods, and explains trusting the \"progeny of the Gods\" as trust in souls who know their leading God and have divinely inspired knowledge, the Orphic tradition passed through Pythagoras.","ideas":["Divine and dæmonic natures exist in both heavens and generation","Telestic statues show matter can share deity","Not all made by junior Gods is mortal, but all mortals are theirs","Inspired knowledge is above both probable and demonstrative knowledge"],"terms":[{"t":"genesiourgoi","d":"\"Fabricators of generation,\" the sublunary Gods."}]},{"i":1572,"j":1577,"loc":"Chapter XXVI","title":"The golden chain and the more excellent genera","summary":"Proclus links the sublunary Gods to the celestial in one golden chain reaching from the intelligible Gods to the last of things. Sublunary Gods keep the father's generative and demiurgic power, each fulfilling one of his wills (connective, prolific, motive or guardian), and are intellectual in essence though mundane in allotment. Between Gods and men he sets angels, dæmons and heroes, analogous to being, life and intellect. Every God leads a multitude of these bearing its own character, Saturnian, Solar or Lunar, just as heroic men took the names of their Gods.","ideas":["The summit of each order is analogous to the order before it","Angels interpret, dæmons govern nature, heroes raise souls","Followers bear the names of the Gods they depend on"],"terms":[{"t":"golden chain","d":"The continuous series of divine orders from intelligibles down to the sublunary Gods."}]},{"i":1578,"j":1585,"loc":"Chapters XXVII–XXVIII","title":"Orphic kingdoms and the sublunary Heaven","summary":"Proclus, following Iamblichus, rejects mythical, civic, ethical and psychic readings of the Timæus genealogy and refers it to Orphic tradition through Pythagoras. He lists the six Orphic kings, Phanes, Night, Heaven, Saturn, Jupiter, Bacchus, whose peculiarities run through every order, and says Plato starts from Hesiod's Heaven and Earth as better known to Greeks. Chapter XXVIII presents Heaven as father and Earth as mother in the world and in each element. After reviewing earlier views of Earth and Heaven, including those of Iamblichus and Theodorus, Proclus makes this Heaven the boundary that brings demiurgic measure to the Gods of generation.","ideas":["Sun and moon mirror Phanes and Night","Plato's ennead is to be seen in every element","Each element is a world with its own Heaven and Earth"],"terms":[{"t":"Ericapæus","d":"Orphic name of Phanes as the first sceptre-bearing king."}]},{"i":1586,"j":1597,"loc":"Chapter XXIX","title":"Earth, marriage, and Ocean and Tethys","summary":"Proclus traces Earth from her appearance among the intellectual Gods; the sublunary Earth is the prolific power of the Heaven of generation, generating infinity as Heaven gives bound. Their union, unlike the united Phanes and Night, is the first marriage, celebrated before the mysteries. From them come Ocean and Tethys, who abide and proceed. After reviewing earlier views and those of Iamblichus, Proclus makes Ocean the cause of all motion and Tethys the cause that separates his streams, giving each motion its purity. He notes Ocean's ten streams, nine bodily, and ends by assigning to this pair what defines and separates.","ideas":["Heaven gives bound; Earth multiplies infinity","Divine generation is union, called marriage, not division","Ocean moves all things; Tethys sorts and settles motions"],"terms":[{"t":"bound and infinity","d":"Paired principles: bound defines each thing, infinity multiplies its powers."}]},{"i":1598,"j":1605,"loc":"Chapter XXX","title":"Phorcys, Saturn and Rhea","summary":"Proclus treats the second progeny, Phorcys, Saturn and Rhea, a triad followed by an unnamed multitude. He meets the problem that Orpheus makes Ocean the brother, not the father, of Saturn: things that proceed first can produce with their causes, and in generation the source of motion rightly comes first. He recounts the Orphic Titans and Ocean's refusal to attack Heaven. Rejecting Theodorus's and partly Iamblichus's placements, he makes Phorcys guardian of spermatic principles, Saturn the divider of forms, and Rhea the giver of life who calls Saturn's powers forth, and names the Titans meant by \"such as subsist together with these.\"","ideas":["Gods can be brothers yet fathers through rank","Ocean abides and proceeds; the other Titans separate","Saturn is the monad, Rhea the duad, Phorcys the perfecter"],"terms":[{"t":"spermatic productive principles","d":"Seed-like formative reasons in nature, over which Phorcys presides."},{"t":"Titans","d":"Children of Heaven and Earth marked by division and progression."}]},{"i":1606,"j":1612,"loc":"Chapter XXXI","title":"Jupiter, Juno, and the sublunary ennead","summary":"Proclus explains the third progression, Jupiter, Juno and their brethren, after reviewing the views of Iamblichus and Theodorus. He distinguishes several Jupiters in Plato and says this Jupiter makes visible Saturn's hidden divisions while Juno brings the evolution of visible forms to perfection. Their brothers rule air, water and earth, and unnamed offspring carry out the partial fabrication. He sums up the ennead: Heaven bounds, Earth strengthens, Ocean moves, Tethys establishes, Saturn divides, Rhea vivifies, Phorcys sows, Jupiter perfects and Juno evolves. He then explains why the ennead and tetrad suit generation, ending with the lowest Gods dividing their works on Saturn's model.","ideas":["Saturn makes intellectual divisions, Rhea psychical, Phorcys physical","Nine proceeds without returning, like generation","The tetrad rules generation: four elements, seasons, centres"],"terms":[{"t":"ennead","d":"The nine sublunary Gods of the Timæus genealogy, completing the fabrication of generation."}]},{"i":1613,"j":1628,"loc":"Chapters XXXII–XXXIII","title":"Sublunary Gods and Bacchus, the mundane intellect","summary":"Why Plato calls the sublunary gods those who appear when they please: they ride in subtler, more divine vehicles beneath the visible elements and show themselves when about to benefit places. Against the Stoics and Numenius, they are unmingled with matter in essence and energy, lead angels, daemons and heroes, and, after Iamblichus, double the celestial rulers in number. The translator then rises to Bacchus, monad of the mundane gods and the world's intellect, torn by the Titans while his heart stays undivided. Proclus' Cratylus scholia on wine as partial intellect and the Orphic reading of the world-soul's mixture close the discussion.","ideas":["Sublunary gods use invisible vehicles prior to the visible elements","Three kinds of intellect: imparticipable, essentially participated, and as a habit","Bacchus torn by the Titans: soul and body divided, the intellectual heart kept whole by Minerva","The world-soul as a mean between impartible intellect and life divided about bodies"],"terms":[{"t":"Bacchus (Dionysus)","d":"Here the mundane intellect and monad of the mundane gods, from whom every partial fabrication depends."},{"t":"Titans","d":"The ultimate fabricators who distribute the Bacchic intellect and soul into the parts of the universe."},{"t":"ochēma (vehicle)","d":"The subtle body in which a divine or rational soul rides and governs grosser bodies."}]},{"i":1629,"j":1634,"loc":"Chapter XXXIV","title":"The equal and unequal in the Parmenides","summary":"The translator quotes the first hypothesis of the Parmenides denying that the one is equal or unequal to itself or another. Since Proclus' commentary on the second hypothesis is lost, he gives Proclus' comment on this passage. Proclus takes equal and unequal as the mark of the mundane gods: equality signals their fullness and fixed bounds, inequality the multitude, excess and defect of their powers and the analogies binding them. The negations are drawn from sameness and the many, and commensurate and incommensurate inequality fit gods who join form to matter and soul to body.","ideas":["Equal and unequal symbolize the mundane gods","The equal ranks under bound, the unequal under infinity","The one is above every opposition, better and worse alike","Commensuration belongs where there is a material subject"],"terms":[{"t":"analogia (proportion)","d":"The bond by which the mundane gods, and Timaeus' world-soul, unite differing powers."},{"t":"peras / apeiria (bound / infinity)","d":"The two coordinations from which the equal and unequal are respectively suspended."}]},{"i":1635,"j":1657,"loc":"Chapters XXXV–XXXVI","title":"Boreas, Pan, Tartarus, Prometheus and other divinities","summary":"Lacking Proclus' seventh book, the translator collects lesser divinities named by Plato. He quotes the Phaedrus on Boreas and Orithya, with Hermeas' comments: an ethical reading of Orithya as a priestess possessed by her god, and a cosmic reading of Boreas as divine providence raising earth's fertility. Socrates is said to reject only physical explanations of myths; Typhon, Achelous and the Nymphs receive their stations. Chapter XXXVI then explains Pan as head of the local gods, Tartarus as the last of each order, Prometheus and Pandora as the descent of the rational soul, Cadmus and Harmonia, and the three kinds of Sirens.","ideas":["Myths may be read historically-ethically and as about wholes","Socrates rejects reducing myths to winds and material causes","Prometheus' stolen fire is the rational soul sent down to earth","Celestial, generative and cathartic Sirens under Jupiter, Neptune and Pluto"],"terms":[{"t":"Typhon","d":"Power presiding over what is confused and disordered at the last procession of things."},{"t":"Pandora","d":"The irrational soul, the feminine medium through which the rational soul joins body."}]},{"i":1658,"j":1667,"loc":"Chapter XXXVII","title":"Nature, Fate and Fortune","summary":"Plato's Nature, the translator says, is neither matter nor body nor soul but a power between soul and bodies, the last cause fabricating the sensible world and suspended from Rhea. Proclus distinguishes three grades of art and calls Nature the organ of the gods. He then rejects the accounts of Fate given by Alexander, Aristotle, Theodorus and Porphyry, defining it as Nature deified and suspended from the demiurgus, whose laws are written in souls; Adrastia, Necessity and Fate form a descending series. Fortune, with Proclus, Sallust and Simplicius, is a divine power ordering scattered, unexpected sublunary events toward good.","ideas":["Nature is art that only proceeds outward, below self-converting soul","Fate is Nature filled with divine illuminations and dependent on the demiurgus","Adrastia, Necessity and Fate: intellectual, supermundane, mundane","Fortune's rudder, globe and horn of Amalthea as emblems"],"terms":[{"t":"heimarmenē (Fate)","d":"The chain and periodic order of many causes moving the sensible world under providence."},{"t":"tuchē (Fortune)","d":"A divine power that orders contingent sublunary affairs to good ends."}]},{"i":1668,"j":1678,"loc":"Chapter XXXVIII","title":"Time, Day and Night, Month and Year as gods","summary":"From Proclus on the Timaeus, Time is described as an intellect that abides by its inward energy yet moves in its external gift, the mean between the merely immovable and soul, which is wholly moved. Hence Plato calls it an eternal image, and its name is glossed as an intellect moving in measure. Day and Night, Month and Year in their primary sense are divine, unapparent measures, of which the visible periods are images; the truer Sun is Time of Time. Proclus adds that these powers were honoured by statues and sacrifice, and neglecting them disorders earthly things.","ideas":["Time is essentially immovable but moved in its participants","Chronos glossed as an intellect dancing in measure","Many orders of Night and Day, apparent and unapparent","Astronomy grasps the ratios of periods only by opinion"],"terms":[{"t":"chronos (Time)","d":"Here a supermundane god, a proceeding intellect that measures every transitive life."},{"t":"aiōn (eternity)","d":"The abiding paradigm of which Time is the moving image."}]},{"i":1679,"j":1687,"loc":"Chapter XXXIX","title":"Divine souls and the one exempt from time","summary":"The translator turns to divine souls and quotes the Parmenides denying that the one is older, younger or the same age, or in time at all. Proclus says the negation produces the order of deified souls from the one and refutes those who make the first cause soul, since every soul participates time; the one, soul and intellect thus appear as three principles. He notes that Parmenides proceeds triadically and to a hexad, fitting soul's triple nature. Divine souls grow older and younger in various respects, and what moves in a circle becomes older and younger than itself at once.","ideas":["Physiologists before Plato thought all things are in time","The triad and hexad suit the order of souls","Souls see true being through time, not eternally","Circular motion makes beginning and end coincide"],"terms":[{"t":"periodos (period)","d":"The circular course of a divine soul, measured by time and returning to its start."}]},{"i":1688,"j":1699,"loc":"Chapters XL–XLI","title":"Love as the great daemon","summary":"Before discussing daemons the translator gives Proclus' account of Love from his commentary on the First Alcibiades. Love converts all things to divine beauty and stands as a medium between lover and beloved, so it is the paradigm of the daemonic order; its series runs from the intelligible gods down through the world. Souls inspired by Love descend to raise others to beauty, while badly reared amatory souls fall into material beauty and a tyrannical life. Proclus' more arcane teaching places Faith, Truth and Love as a triad of monads, and ranks Love in gods, daemons and souls analogous to three intellects.","ideas":["Love is a medium between beloved and lover","Faith, Truth and Love: a triad from the intelligible orders","Perverted love sinks into matter, the sea of dissimilitude","Orpheus calls intelligible Love blind and a great daemon"],"terms":[{"t":"erōs (Love)","d":"The power that turns secondary natures back to beauty and binds them to what is prior."},{"t":"pistis, alētheia, erōs","d":"Faith, Truth and Love, three monads that establish, illuminate and convert all things."}]},{"i":1700,"j":1714,"loc":"Chapters XLII–XLIII","title":"The nature of daemons and our allotted daemons","summary":"The translator argues there must be a middle rank of daemons and heroes between gods and partial souls, holding the gods' gifts in a lesser way. From Proclus he explains that daemons come from Juno with an essence of soul, are ranked under the liberated and mundane gods, and bear their leaders' names. Proclus rejects the views that daemons are dead men's souls, that the planets are daemons, that the rational soul or a part of it is our daemon, or a partial intellect. He lists six grades of daemons and says one allotted daemon governs a person's whole life until judgment.","ideas":["No vacuum among incorporeal natures: a middle genus is needed","Daemons by essence differ from daemons by analogy or habitude","Six grades of daemons from divine to material","The daemon pilots fortune, fate and providence in our life"],"terms":[{"t":"daimōn","d":"A being by essence between gods and men, conveying gifts down and offerings up."},{"t":"kata schesin (by habitude)","d":"Said of a soul that is daemonic by alliance or likeness, not by essence."}]},{"i":1715,"j":1730,"loc":"Chapters XLIV–XLV","title":"Socrates' daemon; angels, daemons and heroes","summary":"Proclus explains three features of Socrates' daemon: it is called a god because it belongs to the highest daemons; its sign was an inward illumination that reached sense as a voice; and it only restrained him, since he was eager to benefit all. This daemon is cathartic, under Apollo, drawing Socrates inward. From the Cratylus scholia Proclus distinguishes angelic, daemonic and heroic genera. The translator adds Hermeas on harmful daemons, Olympiodorus on the six genera and soul-guiding daemons, Proclus on irrational daemons, and souls daemonic only by habitude.","ideas":["Socrates' daemon recalled but never urged","The voice was felt by consciousness rather than sense","Angels unfold the gods' goodness; heroes lead souls upward","No daemon is evil by essence, only by office"],"terms":[{"t":"angeloi (angels)","d":"The genus that makes the gods' uniform nature commensurate with secondary things."},{"t":"hērōes (heroes)","d":"Here the genus that raises souls to the gods through love."}]},{"i":1731,"j":1742,"loc":"Chapter XLVI","title":"Undefiled heroic souls and demigods","summary":"After the attendant genera come human souls of heroic character that descend without defilement; the translator ranks four genera of souls and quotes Plotinus on three classes of men. Hierocles and the Cratylus show such souls called earthly daemons and heroes, with the derivation of hero from love. Proclus explains why fables give heroes divine parents: heroic souls act chiefly by the circle of sameness or of difference, giving a god father or a goddess mother, as Achilles, Minos and Rhadamanthus show. Olympiodorus' report of Plato's descent from Apollo closes the chapter.","ideas":["Four genera of souls, from divine to the wandering many","Good men are daemons as prudent (daēmones)","Sameness and difference circles yield divine father or mother","Heroes descend to benefit fallen souls"],"terms":[{"t":"hēmitheoi (demigods)","d":"Heroic souls with one of their two circles illuminated by the gods."},{"t":"circle of the same / different","d":"Intellectual and opinionative lives of the soul, styled masculine and feminine."}]},{"i":1743,"j":1757,"loc":"Chapters XLVII–XLVIII","title":"Divisions of time; the world a God","summary":"The translator links the Parmenides' division of past, present and future, denied of the one, to angels, daemons and heroes. Proclus divides each tense into a triad, assigns time perfective, connective and unfolding powers received from eternity, and relates souls and the Fates to these. Chapter XLVIII quotes the Timaeus on the world as a blessed god. Proclus maps its wholeness, sphericity and life onto intellectual causes, denies the demiurgus is soul, and reads at a certain time as an essence joined to time, against Atticus.","ideas":["Nine tense-forms in three triads under one monad of time","Time imparts eternity's triple power to souls","The world is temporal and time is mundane","Language divides what divinity produces at once"],"terms":[{"t":"pote (at a certain time)","d":"Not a temporal beginning, but the always-becoming being of the sensible world."}]},{"i":1758,"j":1765,"loc":"Chapter XLIX","title":"Soul placed in the middle of the world","summary":"Proclus says the demiurgus produces all things at once yet in order, and that Plato's account here follows conversion, rising from body to soul and intellect. He surveys interpretations of the middle: earth's centre, moon, sun, fixed sphere, equinoctial or zodiac; Porphyry reads it as soul's middle station, Iamblichus as the supermundane soul. Syrianus' view is preferred: the world-soul has an exempt summit and powers spread through the centre, elements and spheres. The chapter ends contrasting divine and partial souls' bonds with body.","ideas":["Progression and conversion as two orders of exposition","Rival readings of where soul is placed","Plato says soul, not the soul, is set in the middle","The centre as Jupiter's tower or prison, guarded by divine power"],"terms":[{"t":"epistrophē (conversion)","d":"The return of effects to their cause, by which Plato orders this passage."},{"t":"Ippa","d":"Orphic name for the world-soul, whose head is its supermundane summit."}]},{"i":1766,"j":1785,"loc":"Chapters L–LI","title":"One, only, solitary: the world's names","summary":"Following Porphyry and Iamblichus, Proclus explains circle revolving in a circle as the bodily circle moved within the soul's. The three names one, only and solitary come from the final, paradigmatic and demiurgic causes, so the world is a god; its self-sufficiency is compared to the virtuous man's friendship with himself. Virtue, self-knowledge and friendship come from soul, intellect and deity, and Proclus ranks six forms of felicity. On the names heaven and world, he calls the universe a statue of the intelligible, holding an ineffable divine name for its abiding.","ideas":["One, only, solitary from three causes","The vicious flee themselves; the good love themselves","Six forms of felicity, from the world to irrational animals","Divine names differ from human names, as Xanthus and Scamander"],"terms":[{"t":"autarkeia (self-sufficiency)","d":"The world's needing nothing external, from abundance of power."},{"t":"kosmos / ouranos","d":"World as proceeding from true beings; heaven as turning back to them."}]},{"i":1786,"j":1929,"loc":"Endnotes","title":"Translator's endnotes and textual corrections","summary":"The endnotes give references to Sallust, Iamblichus' On the Mysteries and Proclus on the Timaeus, with many emendations to the Greek of the original edition. Longer notes explain the Chaldean seven corporeal worlds, why every sphere contains all the gods, the Pythagorean ennead, and the thirty-six decadarchs. Others quote the Emerald Table attributed to Hermes Trismegistus in Latin and English and Jeremiah on the queen of heaven. Notes also explain the Phaedrus chariot and Plato's ten kinds of motion, and point to a defence of Achilles.","ideas":["Each sphere holds every god in its own character","The ennead as Ocean and horizon of number","As above, so below: the Emerald Table","Many corrections to the printed Greek"],"terms":[{"t":"decadarchs","d":"Thirty-six divine rulers dividing the zodiac, mentioned also by Julian."}]}]});